// Schema: {q,o,a,e} with 5 options A-E, answer index 0-4
if(!window.MM) window.MM={};
window.MM['radio_golden_2026_apr8_evening']=[
/*1*/ {"q":"Which of the following chemotherapy drugs acts by inhibiting microtubule assembly?","o":["Cyclophosphamide","Methotrexate","Vinblastine","Cisplatin","Paclitaxel"],"a":2,"e":"Vinblastine binds to tubulin to prevent its polymerization into microtubules, effectively halting the cell cycle in mitosis. Used in lymphoma and testicular cancer treatment.<br><br>Take Notes: Cyclophosphamide → alkylating agent | Methotrexate → antimetabolite | Paclitaxel → stabilizes microtubules | Microtubule assembly inhibitor = vinblastine."},
/*2*/ {"q":"To study the relationship between blood pressure and height in a sample of 200 people, the most appropriate graphical method is:","o":["Bar chart","Histogram","Pie chart","Scatter plot","Box and whisker plot"],"a":3,"e":"Scatter plot is the standard tool to visualize the correlation and relationship between two continuous numerical variables. Each dot represents one individual's paired values for BP and height.<br><br>Take Notes: Bar chart → categorical data | Histogram → frequency distribution | Pie chart → proportions | Two continuous variables = scatter plot."},
/*3*/ {"q":"A young child presents with recurrent respiratory infections, chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis and dextrocardia. Most likely diagnosis?","o":["Cystic Fibrosis","Kartagener syndrome","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Laryngomalacia","Immotile cilia syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Kartagener syndrome is a primary ciliary dyskinesia characterized by the classic triad of situs inversus (dextrocardia), bronchiectasis, and sinusitis. Defective dynein arms impair ciliary motility throughout the body.<br><br>Take Notes: Cystic fibrosis → no situs inversus | Alpha-1 antitrypsin → emphysema | Classic triad with dextrocardia = Kartagener."},
/*4*/ {"q":"A patient presents with cheilosis and corneal vascularization. Most likely vitamin deficiency?","o":["Thiamine","Riboflavin","Niacin","Pyridoxine","Cobalamin"],"a":1,"e":"Riboflavin (vitamin B2) deficiency uniquely presents with cheilosis (cracks at mouth corners), angular stomatitis, glossitis, and corneal neovascularization. Needed for FAD and FMN coenzyme synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Thiamine → beriberi and Wernicke syndrome | Niacin → pellagra (4 Ds) | Pyridoxine → peripheral neuropathy | Cheilosis + corneal vascularization = riboflavin."},
/*5*/ {"q":"What is formed by the fusion of the spinous processes of the sacrum?","o":["Lateral sacral crest","Medial sacral crest","Median sacral crest","Sacral hiatus","Sacral promontory"],"a":2,"e":"Adjacent spinous processes of sacrum fuse to form the median sacral crest lying in the midline posteriorly. Failure of fusion of S4 and S5 spinous processes creates the sacral hiatus inferiorly.<br><br>Take Notes: Lateral crest → fused transverse processes | Sacral hiatus → S4-S5 fusion failure | Sacral promontory → anterior S1 | Fused spinous processes = median sacral crest."},
/*6*/ {"q":"A patient with suspected rotator cuff injury undergoes a shoulder arthrogram. After contrast injection into the glenohumeral joint, the dye extends into a structure between the scapula neck and subscapularis tendon. Which bursa communicates?","o":["Subacromial bursa","Subdeltoid bursa","Subscapularis bursa","Infraspinatus bursa","Subcoracoid bursa"],"a":2,"e":"Subscapularis bursa consistently communicates with the glenohumeral joint cavity via an opening in the anterior capsule called the foramen of Weitbrecht. Contrast injected into the joint readily fills this bursa.<br><br>Take Notes: Subacromial-subdeltoid → separate bursa above supraspinatus | Infraspinatus bursa → posterior | Communicates with shoulder joint = subscapularis bursa."},
/*7*/ {"q":"The medial cord of the brachial plexus continues as which of the following nerves?","o":["Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm","Ulnar nerve","Medial cutaneous nerve of arm","Musculocutaneous nerve","Median nerve"],"a":1,"e":"Ulnar nerve originates from the C8 and T1 roots and serves as the direct terminal continuation of the medial cord of the brachial plexus. The medial cord also gives the medial root of median and the medial cutaneous nerves.<br><br>Take Notes: Musculocutaneous → lateral cord | Median → medial + lateral cord roots | Medial cord terminal continuation = ulnar nerve."},
/*8*/ {"q":"A patient with a medial epicondyle injury cannot abduct or adduct the fingers and cannot grip paper between fingers (Froment's sign). Which nerve is injured?","o":["Median nerve","Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Axillary nerve"],"a":2,"e":"Ulnar nerve innervates the interossei and adductor pollicis muscles, which are necessary for finger abduction, adduction, and strong grip. Medial epicondyle injury damages the ulnar nerve as it travels through the cubital tunnel.<br><br>Take Notes: Median → thenar weakness (LOAF muscles) | Radial → wrist drop | Froment's sign + medial epicondyle injury = ulnar nerve."},
/*9*/ {"q":"A young boy has an injury on the back of his wrist. Which muscles are located within the first extensor compartment?","o":["Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis","Abductor pollicis longus and Extensor pollicis brevis","Extensor pollicis longus","Extensor digitorum and Extensor indicis","Extensor carpi ulnaris"],"a":1,"e":"First dorsal compartment of the wrist contains abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB). These tendons are involved in De Quervain tenosynovitis. Six compartments lie deep to the extensor retinaculum.<br><br>Take Notes: 2nd compartment → ECRL + ECRB | 3rd compartment → EPL | 6th compartment → ECU | First extensor compartment = APL + EPB."},
/*10*/ {"q":"A patient presents with bone pain. Laboratory investigations show: low serum calcium, low phosphate, high PTH, high alkaline phosphatase. Most important diagnosis?","o":["Vitamin D deficiency","Primary Hyperparathyroidism","Renal Impairment","Acute respiratory acidosis","Hypoparathyroidism"],"a":0,"e":"Vitamin D deficiency presents with low calcium and low phosphate, secondary hyperparathyroidism (elevated PTH compensating for hypocalcemia), and raised alkaline phosphatase from osteomalacia. This is the most likely diagnosis given the full laboratory panel.<br><br>Take Notes: Primary hyperparathyroidism → high calcium | Renal impairment → high phosphate | Hypoparathyroidism → low PTH | Low Ca + low PO4 + high PTH/ALP = vitamin D deficiency."},
/*11*/ {"q":"Which of the following drugs is most commonly associated with orthostatic hypotension?","o":["Alpha blocker","Nitrous Oxide","Non-selective beta blocker","Selective beta blocker","Calcium channel blocker"],"a":0,"e":"Alpha blockers (prazosin, doxazosin, tamsulosin) inhibit vascular alpha-1 receptors, preventing the compensatory vasoconstriction needed to maintain blood pressure when standing. Causes first-dose syncope and postural hypotension.<br><br>Take Notes: Beta blockers → bradycardia not postural drop | Calcium channel blockers → flushing and edema | Orthostatic hypotension = alpha blocker."},
/*12*/ {"q":"A female undergoes radical mastectomy with axillary lymph node dissection. Post-operatively her scapula protrudes backward when pushing against a wall and cannot abduct arm above 90 degrees. Nerve injured?","o":["Axillary nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Medial pectoral nerve","Intercostobrachial nerve"],"a":2,"e":"Long thoracic nerve injury (during axillary surgery) causes serratus anterior denervation and inability to upwardly rotate the scapula, producing a winged scapula and inability to abduct the arm above 90 degrees.<br><br>Take Notes: Axillary → deltoid shoulder abduction 0-90 | Thoracodorsal → latissimus dorsi | Winged scapula + inability to abduct above 90 = long thoracic nerve."},
/*13*/ {"q":"Which of the following structures is an anterior relation to the third part of the duodenum?","o":["Superior mesenteric artery (SMA)","Superior mesenteric vein (SMV)","Inferior mesenteric artery","Common bile duct","Gastroduodenal artery"],"a":0,"e":"Superior mesenteric artery and vein cross anterior to the third (horizontal) part of the duodenum. Compression here by SMA against the aorta produces SMA syndrome with proximal duodenal obstruction.<br><br>Take Notes: Common bile duct → posterior to D1 | Inferior mesenteric artery → lower retroperitoneum | Gastroduodenal artery → posterior to D1 | Anterior to D3 = SMA and SMV."},
/*14*/ {"q":"The most common site for a carcinoid tumor is:","o":["Appendix","Small intestine","Rectum","Stomach","Lung"],"a":0,"e":"Appendix is the most common site for carcinoid (neuroendocrine) tumors, especially the tip. Usually small, indolent, and incidentally found. Small bowel (ileum) is the second most common site overall.<br><br>Take Notes: Small bowel → second commonest, often metastasizes | Rectum → usually benign | Lung → bronchial carcinoid | Commonest GI carcinoid = appendix."},
/*15*/ {"q":"Which specialized cells in the small intestine are responsible for antigen uptake and presentation to lymphoid tissue?","o":["Paneth cells","Goblet cells","Enterocytes","M cells","S cells"],"a":3,"e":"M cells (microfold cells) are specialized epithelial cells located in Peyer patches. They sample luminal antigens and present them to underlying immune cells, acting as a gateway for antigens to reach the immune system.<br><br>Take Notes: Paneth → antimicrobial defensins | Goblet → mucus production | Enterocytes → nutrient absorption | Antigen sampling and presentation = M cells."},
/*16*/ {"q":"Secretin is primarily produced and secreted by which organ?","o":["Pancreas","Bladder","Stomach","Small intestine","Liver"],"a":3,"e":"Secretin is secreted by S cells of the duodenum (small intestine) in response to acidic chyme entering from the stomach. It stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and inhibits gastric acid secretion.<br><br>Take Notes: Pancreas → digestive enzymes and insulin | Stomach → gastrin from G cells | Liver → bile production | Secretin source = duodenal S cells (small intestine)."},
/*17*/ {"q":"Which of the following structures is posteriorly located to the third part of the duodenum?","o":["Right Ureter","Right Psoas muscle","Inferior vena cava (IVC)","Aorta","Head of Pancreas"],"a":3,"e":"Third part of the duodenum lies anterior to the aorta and IVC. Among the options, the aorta is the major retroperitoneal structure directly posterior to D3. The SMA crosses anteriorly creating the SMA angle.<br><br>Take Notes: Right ureter → lateral to IVC | Right psoas muscle → lateral | Inferior vena cava → posterior to D2 mainly | D3 posterior = aorta."},
/*18*/ {"q":"Which part(s) of the duodenum are considered retroperitoneal?","o":["Coils of jejunum","All except the first part","Only the second part","Only the third and fourth parts","All four parts"],"a":1,"e":"Only the proximal portion (first 2 cm) of the duodenum is intraperitoneal. The rest of the duodenum (D2, D3, D4) is secondarily retroperitoneal, fixed against the posterior abdominal wall by fusion fascia.<br><br>Take Notes: D1 → mostly intraperitoneal | D2-D4 → secondarily retroperitoneal | Retroperitoneal duodenum = all except first part."},
/*19*/ {"q":"What is the most serious side effect of estrogen-only therapy in postmenopausal women with an intact uterus?","o":["Endometrial carcinoma","Bladder cancer","Breast cancer","Osteoporosis","Hot flashes"],"a":0,"e":"Unopposed estrogen therapy leads to excessive proliferation of the uterine lining, which significantly increases the risk of endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma. Progesterone is added to oppose this effect in women with intact uterus.<br><br>Take Notes: Breast cancer → combined HRT raises risk modestly | Osteoporosis → estrogen protects bone | Unopposed estrogen + intact uterus = endometrial carcinoma risk."},
/*20*/ {"q":"A female presents with forefoot pain. Radiographic imaging reveals avascular necrosis of a metatarsal head. This condition, Freiberg's disease, most commonly involves:","o":["1st Metatarsal","2nd Metatarsal","3rd Metatarsal","5th Metatarsal","Calcaneus"],"a":1,"e":"Freiberg's disease is avascular necrosis most commonly affecting the head of the second metatarsal due to its length and rigidity. Common in young women who wear high heels. Pain over the metatarsal head with weight-bearing.<br><br>Take Notes: 1st metatarsal → bunion (hallux valgus) | 5th metatarsal → Jones fracture | Freiberg's disease = 2nd metatarsal head."},
/*21*/ {"q":"A patient presents with pain and significant blood pressure discrepancy between the two arms. Retrograde blood flow is noted in the vertebral artery. Most likely diagnosis?","o":["Coarctation of the aorta","Subclavian steal syndrome","Thoracic outlet syndrome","Takayasu's arteritis","Cervical rib syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Subclavian steal syndrome is proximal subclavian stenosis causing retrograde flow in the ipsilateral vertebral artery to supply the arm, stealing blood from the cerebral circulation. Causes dizziness and vertigo with arm exercise.<br><br>Take Notes: Coarctation → upper limb > lower limb BP | Takayasu's → pulseless disease | Retrograde vertebral artery flow = subclavian steal syndrome."},
/*22*/ {"q":"The radial artery crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox resting on scaphoid and trapezium bones, vulnerable to deep lacerations. Which artery is this?","o":["Ulnar artery","Radial artery","Cephalic vein","Median nerve","Radial nerve"],"a":1,"e":"The radial artery crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox where it lies on the scaphoid and trapezium bones. It is vulnerable to deep snuffbox lacerations and is used for radial artery pulse palpation.<br><br>Take Notes: Ulnar artery → Guyon canal | Cephalic vein → lateral arm | Median nerve → carpal tunnel | Snuffbox floor artery = radial."},
/*23*/ {"q":"A 65-year-old patient with non-valvular atrial fibrillation is started on an oral anticoagulant medication. Which of the following is the best choice?","o":["Warfarin only","Apixaban","Dabigatran","Rivaroxaban","Enoxaparin"],"a":1,"e":"Apixaban is a Factor Xa inhibitor often preferred in non-valvular atrial fibrillation for its favorable balance of stroke prevention and lower bleeding risk compared to other anticoagulants. Belongs to the NOAC class.<br><br>Take Notes: Dabigatran → direct thrombin inhibitor | Rivaroxaban → Factor Xa (higher bleeding risk) | Enoxaparin → low molecular weight heparin parenteral | Best NOAC for non-valvular AF = apixaban."},
/*24*/ {"q":"Thiazide diuretics exert their primary effect at which site in the nephron?","o":["Early distal convoluted tubule (DCT)","Collecting duct (CD)","Thick ascending limb of the Loop of Henle","Proximal convoluted tubule","Descending limb of the Loop of Henle"],"a":0,"e":"Thiazides inhibit the Na+/Cl- co-transporter (NCC) in the early distal convoluted tubule, preventing reabsorption of sodium and chloride. Causes calcium retention (useful in osteoporosis and idiopathic hypercalciuria).<br><br>Take Notes: Loop diuretics → thick ascending limb (NKCC2) | Spironolactone → collecting duct | Thiazide primary site = early DCT."},
/*25*/ {"q":"Glucose enters the enterocyte from the lumen of the small intestine primarily via which mechanism?","o":["Na+ co-transport","Facilitated diffusion","Simple diffusion","Primary active transport","Solvent drag"],"a":0,"e":"Glucose enters the enterocyte via secondary active transport through the SGLT1 transporter, which is driven by the sodium gradient established by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase. This couples glucose absorption to sodium reabsorption.<br><br>Take Notes: Facilitated diffusion → GLUT2 at basolateral exit | Solvent drag → minor role | Intestinal glucose entry = Na+ co-transport (SGLT1)."},
/*26*/ {"q":"A patient presents with sudden, severe headache. Lumbar puncture reveals blood in the cerebrospinal fluid with a yellowish tint (Xanthochromia). Most likely diagnosis?","o":["Epidural hematoma","Subdural hematoma","Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH)","Traumatic lumbar puncture","Chronic subdural hematoma"],"a":2,"e":"Xanthochromia is the yellowish tint in CSF resulting from hemoglobin breakdown into bilirubin, confirming the presence of blood in the subarachnoid space for several hours. This is the hallmark of SAH after CT negative.<br><br>Take Notes: Epidural → lens-shaped, middle meningeal artery | Subdural → crescent, bridging veins | Xanthochromia in CSF = subarachnoid hemorrhage."},
/*27*/ {"q":"In axonal injury, what undergoes characteristic changes such as fragmentation and disappearance within the cell body (chromatolysis)?","o":["Nissl bodies","Nissl granules","Axon hillock","Golgi apparatus","Centrosome"],"a":0,"e":"Chromatolysis is the histological response to axonal injury where Nissl bodies (rough endoplasmic reticulum) disperse as the cell shifts to protein synthesis for repair. Nissl granules are the discrete clumps that undergo fragmentation.<br><br>Take Notes: Axon hillock → origin of action potential | Nissl substance → broader collective term | Chromatolysis target = Nissl bodies (granules)."},
/*28*/ {"q":"The flexor retinaculum (transverse carpal ligament) of the hand is attached medially to which of the following bones?","o":["Pisiform","Scaphoid","Trapezium","Lunate","Capitate"],"a":0,"e":"The flexor retinaculum attaches medially to the pisiform and the hook of the hamate, forming the roof of the carpal tunnel. The lateral attachments are to the scaphoid and trapezium tubercles.<br><br>Take Notes: Lateral attachment → scaphoid + trapezium tubercles | Median nerve runs deep to ligament | Flexor retinaculum medial = pisiform + hamate hook."},
/*29*/ {"q":"The xiphisternal joint is typically located at which vertebral level?","o":["T7","T8","T9","T10","T12"],"a":2,"e":"The xiphisternal joint is a key anatomical landmark that typically aligns with the level of the T9 vertebral body. It is useful for surface anatomy when locating the inferior thoracic structures.<br><br>Take Notes: Sternal angle → T4/T5 vertebral level | Jugular notch → T2/T3 vertebral level | Xiphisternal joint = T9 vertebral level."},
/*30*/ {"q":"In Hirschsprung aganglionic megacolon, which part of the gastrointestinal tract is almost always involved?","o":["Rectum","Sigmoid colon","Descending colon","Ascending colon","Terminal ileum"],"a":0,"e":"Hirschsprung disease results from the failure of neural crest cells to migrate caudally, so the distal rectum is always affected. The aganglionic segment begins at the anus and extends proximally in varying lengths.<br><br>Take Notes: Sigmoid colon → second commonest extension | Terminal ileum → very rare total colonic aganglionosis | Hirschsprung always involves = rectum."},
/*31*/ {"q":"The epiarterial bronchus refers specifically to which of the following?","o":["The left main bronchus","The right superior lobe bronchus","The right middle lobe bronchus","The right inferior lobe bronchus","The bronchus intermedius"],"a":1,"e":"The right superior lobe bronchus is termed epiarterial because it is the only bronchus that passes superior to (above) the pulmonary artery. All other bronchi pass below the artery (hyparterial).<br><br>Take Notes: Bronchus intermedius → continues below right upper lobe takeoff | Left main bronchus → entirely hyparterial | Epiarterial bronchus = right superior lobe bronchus."},
/*32*/ {"q":"Which of the following pressure differences is primarily responsible for the distension of the lung during inspiration?","o":["Intrapulmonary pressure","Transpulmonary pressure","Intrapleural pressure","Atmospheric pressure","Transdiaphragmatic pressure"],"a":1,"e":"Transpulmonary pressure (alveolar pressure minus intrapleural pressure) is the distending pressure across the lung wall, directly responsible for lung expansion during inspiration. When it exceeds elastic recoil, the lung expands.<br><br>Take Notes: Intrapulmonary pressure → alveolar (zero at end inspiration) | Intrapleural pressure → negative during inspiration | Lung distension driver = transpulmonary pressure."},
/*33*/ {"q":"What is the primary consequence of a deficiency in pulmonary surfactant in a premature neonate with Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome?","o":["Alveolar collapse (atelectasis)","Increased lung compliance","Decreased surface tension","Pulmonary hypertension","Decreased work of breathing"],"a":0,"e":"Surfactant reduces alveolar surface tension; its deficiency causes high tension that leads to alveolar collapse (atelectasis) and respiratory distress. Compliance falls significantly and work of breathing rises sharply.<br><br>Take Notes: Compliance → decreased not increased | Work of breathing → increased not decreased | Surfactant deficiency consequence = alveolar collapse."},
/*34*/ {"q":"An unconscious patient aspirates gastric contents. In which specific anatomical location is the material most likely to settle?","o":["Lateral segment of the right lower lobe","Superior segment of the right lower lobe","Apical segment of the right upper lobe","Posterior segment of the right upper lobe","Right middle lobe"],"a":1,"e":"For a supine patient, aspirated material is most dependent in the superior segment of the right lower lobe (also possible in posterior segment of right upper lobe). Right bronchus is more vertical and wider than left.<br><br>Take Notes: Upright patient → right lower lobe basal segments | Patient lying on right side → right upper lobe posterior | Supine aspiration = superior segment RLL."},
/*35*/ {"q":"A patient has a posterolateral disc herniation between the C5 and C6 vertebrae. Which spinal nerve root is most likely to be compressed?","o":["C4","C5","C6","C7","T1"],"a":2,"e":"Cervical nerve roots exit above the corresponding vertebra (C6 exits between C5-C6). A posterolateral disc herniation laterally affects the C6 nerve root in cervical levels. Causes thumb numbness and biceps weakness.<br><br>Take Notes: C5 root → deltoid weakness, lateral arm sensation | C7 root → triceps weakness, middle finger sensation | C5-C6 disc herniation affects = C6 root."},
/*36*/ {"q":"The liver bud arises from the foregut endoderm, giving rise to hepatocytes and the entire epithelial lining of the biliary system. From which embryonic germ layer is it derived?","o":["Neural crest","Endoderm","Ectoderm","Lateral plate mesoderm","Splanchnic mesoderm"],"a":1,"e":"Hepatocytes and the entire epithelial lining of the biliary tree (intra- and extrahepatic bile ducts) are derived from the foregut endoderm. Hepatic stellate cells and Kupffer cells arise from mesoderm.<br><br>Take Notes: Neural crest → peripheral nervous system and enteric neurons | Mesoderm → connective tissue and blood vessels | Liver epithelium origin = endoderm."},
/*37*/ {"q":"A patient experiences intense generalized itching (pruritus) immediately after a hot shower. His hemoglobin is 19. This finding is most strongly associated with:","o":["Polycythemia vera","Essential thrombocythemia","Chronic myeloid leukemia","Aplastic anemia","Multiple myeloma"],"a":0,"e":"Aquagenic pruritus (itching after hot bath) combined with elevated hemoglobin is a classic diagnostic clue for polycythemia vera. JAK2 mutation drives erythrocytosis and basophil/mast cell histamine release.<br><br>Take Notes: Essential thrombocythemia → elevated platelets | Chronic myeloid leukemia → elevated WBC, Philadelphia chromosome | Multiple myeloma → no characteristic pruritus | Aquagenic itch + high Hb = polycythemia vera."},
/*38*/ {"q":"A patient with sickle cell disease presents with worsening jaundice, hemoglobin drop, and rapidly enlarging painful spleen. Most likely cause?","o":["Vaso-occlusive crisis","Splenic sequestration crisis","Aplastic crisis","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Hyperhemolytic crisis"],"a":1,"e":"Splenic sequestration is a life-threatening crisis in sickle cell patients where large volumes of blood are trapped in the spleen, causing acute anemia and splenomegaly. Most common in young children before autosplenectomy.<br><br>Take Notes: Vaso-occlusive → pain crisis not splenic enlargement | Aplastic → parvovirus B19 infection | Hemolytic → no big tender spleen | Big tender spleen + Hb drop = splenic sequestration."},
/*39*/ {"q":"A patient presents with stroke involving the right face, arm, and leg. No sensory deficits, speech and vision intact. Most likely lesion site?","o":["Left precentral gyrus (Motor cortex)","Right internal capsule","Left internal capsule","Left pons","Right medulla"],"a":2,"e":"A pure motor stroke involving the face, arm, and leg without sensory loss localizes to the contralateral (left) posterior limb of the internal capsule. This is a typical lacunar infarct from small vessel disease.<br><br>Take Notes: Cortical motor stroke → face or arm more than leg weakness | Pons stroke → cranial nerve signs also present | Pure motor hemiparesis = internal capsule (contralateral)."},
/*40*/ {"q":"A patient has injury of the right anterior spinothalamic tract. Which of the following functions will be affected?","o":["Vibration and proprioception","Pain and temperature","Light touch and pressure","Crude touch and pressure","Voluntary motor control"],"a":3,"e":"The anterior spinothalamic tract carries crude (light) touch and pressure sensations to the brain. Lesion causes contralateral loss of these modalities; lateral spinothalamic carries pain and temperature.<br><br>Take Notes: Lateral spinothalamic tract → pain and temperature sensation | Dorsal columns → vibration, proprioception, and fine touch | Anterior spinothalamic = crude touch and pressure."},
/*41*/ {"q":"A multiparous woman presents with bone pain and muscle weakness. Labs: low calcium, low vitamin D, high alkaline phosphatase. Symptoms improve with calcium and vitamin D supplementation. Likely diagnosis?","o":["Osteoporosis","Osteomalacia","Paget's disease of bone","Osteitis fibrosa cystica","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],"a":1,"e":"Osteomalacia is characterized by defective bone mineralization due to vitamin D deficiency, typically showing low calcium, elevated alkaline phosphatase, and clinical response to vitamin D. Common in multiparous women.<br><br>Take Notes: Osteoporosis → normal laboratory values | Paget's disease → normal calcium with high alkaline phosphatase | Osteitis fibrosa cystica → high calcium and PTH | Low Ca/vitD + ALP high = osteomalacia."},
/*42*/ {"q":"A patient undergoes surgery for a long bone fracture. Three weeks post-operatively develops a pulmonary embolism (PE). Most likely embolus source?","o":["Deep calf veins","Femoral vein","Great saphenous vein","Popliteal vein","Internal iliac vein"],"a":0,"e":"Proximal deep vein thrombi are significantly more likely to embolize to the lungs than those in distal calf veins. Long-bone fracture is a major risk factor for DVT formation in the proximal veins.<br><br>Take Notes: Great saphenous vein → superficial venous system | Internal iliac vein → less common source | PE source = proximal deep veins (femoral, popliteal > deep calf)."},
/*43*/ {"q":"Regarding Free Fatty Acids (FFAs) in the bloodstream, which of the following is true?","o":["They are metabolically inert","They are unaffected by epinephrine","They remain bound to albumin","They are water-soluble molecules","They cannot be used by the heart for energy"],"a":2,"e":"Because they are hydrophobic (water-insoluble), free fatty acids must be transported in the blood bound to the protein albumin. Epinephrine stimulates lipolysis releasing FFAs from adipose tissue.<br><br>Take Notes: Heart and skeletal muscle → use FFAs avidly for energy | Epinephrine → increases plasma FFA levels | Bloodstream FFAs = albumin-bound."},
/*44*/ {"q":"What is the exact mechanism of action of Thiazolidinediones (e.g., Pioglitazone, Rosiglitazone)?","o":["Activation of PPAR-gamma nuclear receptor","Inhibition of dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4)","Inhibition of alpha-glucosidase in the brush border","Stimulation of pancreatic beta cells","Inhibition of sodium-glucose co-transporter"],"a":0,"e":"Thiazolidinediones activate PPAR-gamma agonists that enter the nucleus to modulate the transcription of genes involved in glucose and lipid metabolism, increasing insulin sensitivity dramatically.<br><br>Take Notes: DPP-4 inhibitors → sitagliptin mechanism | Acarbose → alpha-glucosidase inhibitor | SGLT2 inhibitors → empagliflozin mechanism | Thiazolidinediones MOA = PPAR-gamma agonism."},
/*45*/ {"q":"The glycocalyx found on the outer surface of plasma membranes is composed primarily of:","o":["Carbohydrate","Phospholipid bilayers","Cholesterol and fatty acids","Cytoskeletal proteins like actin","Nucleic acid chains"],"a":0,"e":"The glycocalyx is a coat on the cell surface made of glycoproteins and glycolipids that aids in cell-cell recognition and protection. It is composed of carbohydrate chains attached to membrane proteins and lipids.<br><br>Take Notes: Phospholipid bilayer → main membrane structure | Cytoskeleton → cytoplasmic side of membrane | Glycocalyx composition = carbohydrate chains."},
/*46*/ {"q":"A patient suffered a myocardial infarction one month ago. If examined today, what would be the predominant finding in the affected myocardium?","o":["Fibrous scar","Complete resolution (regeneration of myocytes)","Coagulative necrosis","Granulation tissue","Contraction bands"],"a":0,"e":"By four weeks post-MI, the necrotic tissue has been replaced by type I collagen, forming a dense fibrous scar. Earlier stages include: coagulation necrosis (1-3 days), neutrophil infiltration (1-3 days), granulation tissue (1-3 weeks).<br><br>Take Notes: Coagulative necrosis → 1-3 days post-MI | Granulation tissue → 1-3 weeks post-MI | One month post-MI = fibrous scar formation."},
/*47*/ {"q":"A patient admitted for acute MI dies suddenly within first hours despite appropriate treatment. Most likely cause of death?","o":["Ventricular rupture","Papillary muscle rupture","Acute septal rupture","Ventricular fibrillation/arrhythmia","Dressler syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Ventricular fibrillation and re-entry arrhythmias from ischemic myocardium cause sudden cardiac arrest and death in the first few hours following acute MI. This is the most common early cause of sudden cardiac death.<br><br>Take Notes: Ventricular rupture → occurs 5-14 days post-MI | Papillary muscle rupture → occurs 2-7 days post-MI | Sudden death early post-MI = ventricular fibrillation/arrhythmia."},
/*48*/ {"q":"A 60-year-old smoker with painless hematuria has atypical urothelial cells on cytology. Cystoscopy shows no visible bladder lesions. Most likely diagnosis?","o":["Squamous cell carcinoma of bladder","Cystitis cystica","Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)","Acute pyelonephritis","Urothelial carcinoma of upper urinary tract"],"a":4,"e":"If malignant urothelial cells are present but the bladder appears normal on cystoscopy, the primary tumor is likely located in the upper urinary tract (renal pelvis or ureter). Carcinoma in situ also possible.<br><br>Take Notes: Squamous cell carcinoma → schistosomiasis association | Cystitis cystica → benign condition | Painless hematuria + smoking + abnormal cytology = upper tract urothelial CA."},
/*49*/ {"q":"A factory worker with a rare aggressive malignant vascular tumor is most classically associated with exposure to:","o":["Vinyl chloride","Arsenic","Asbestos","Benzene","Formaldehyde"],"a":0,"e":"Hepatic angiosarcoma, a rare and highly aggressive malignant vascular tumor, is most classically associated with chronic exposure to vinyl chloride (in plastic factory workers). Also linked to arsenic and Thorotrast.<br><br>Take Notes: Aromatic amines → bladder cancer association | Arsenic → lung, skin, and liver cancer | Asbestos → mesothelioma and lung cancer | Vinyl chloride + factory = hepatic angiosarcoma."},
/*50*/ {"q":"A woman with SLE is pregnant. Which antibodies are most strongly associated with permanent fetal cardiac anomaly (congenital heart block)?","o":["Anti-Smith (Anti-Sm)","Anti-Ro (Anti-SSA) and Anti-La (Anti-SSB)","Anti-dsDNA","Anti-histone","Anti-U1 RNP"],"a":1,"e":"Anti-Ro (SSA) and Anti-La (SSB) antibodies can cross the placenta. They are specifically linked to neonatal lupus and the development of permanent congenital heart block due to effects on fetal cardiac conduction.<br><br>Take Notes: Anti-Smith → SLE specific (no fetal heart block association) | Anti-dsDNA → SLE disease activity marker | Anti-histone → drug-induced lupus marker | Neonatal heart block = anti-Ro/La."},
/*51*/ {"q":"A Type II (Delayed-type) hypersensitivity reaction test. The positive skin reaction observed 48 to 72 hours after administration of a PPD test is primarily mediated by which of the following cell types?","o":["Neutrophils","B lymphocytes and plasma cells","T cells","Natural Killer (NK) cells","Eosinophils"],"a":2,"e":"PPD (tuberculin) skin test is a classic example of Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity mediated by sensitized CD4+ T cells (Th1); they release cytokines recruiting macrophages causing induration 48-72 hours later.<br><br>Take Notes: Type I → IgE + mast cells | Type II → antibody-mediated cytotoxicity | Type III → immune complexes | PPD = Type IV, T-cell mediated."},
/*52*/ {"q":"A patient is contaminated wound with a high-risk process is analyzed using gel electrophoresis to evaluate the state of its nuclear DNA. Which of the following DNA fragmentation patterns is most characteristic of malignant cells occupying the full diameter?","o":["Step-ladder pattern","Circular pattern","Punctuated pattern","No fragmentation","Smear pattern"],"a":4,"e":"Necrosis produces a smear pattern on gel electrophoresis due to random DNA degradation at multiple sites. Apoptosis produces the classic ladder pattern (internucleosomal 180 bp fragments). Malignant necrotic tissue typically shows smear.<br><br>Take Notes: Ladder pattern → apoptosis (caspase-activated DNase) | Random degradation → necrosis | Malignant necrosis gel pattern = smear."},
/*53*/ {"q":"A patient presents with an unknown vaccination history and is administered Tetanus Immune Globulin (TIG). What type of immunity does this provide?","o":["Active immunity","Passive immunity","Innate immunity","Humoral immunity","Cell-mediated immunity"],"a":1,"e":"Tetanus Immune Globulin (TIG) provides passive immunity by delivering pre-formed antibodies directly. It offers immediate but temporary protection lasting weeks, unlike active immunization which stimulates the body's own antibody production.<br><br>Take Notes: Active immunity → vaccines (long-lasting) | Innate → non-specific, no memory | TIG = passive immunity (pre-formed antibodies)."},
/*54*/ {"q":"A 35-year-old patient with a persistent cough and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy reveals the presence of non-caseating granulomas. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Tuberculosis","Histoplasmosis","Sarcoidosis","Cat scratch disease","Silicosis"],"a":2,"e":"Sarcoidosis classically presents with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, non-caseating granulomas, and restrictive lung disease. Common in young adults; granulomas contain lymphocytes and multinucleated giant cells without central caseation.<br><br>Take Notes: TB → caseating granulomas | Histoplasmosis → caseating + fungal | Cat scratch → Bartonella | Bilateral hilar + non-caseating = sarcoidosis."},
/*55*/ {"q":"A patient, a non-HIV individual, presents with a low-grade fever, a non-productive cough with bilateral interstitial infiltrates, and a thick, a non, with blister fever. What is the most likely causative organism?","o":["Pneumocystis jirovecii","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Cryptococcus neoformans","Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"],"a":0,"e":"Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP) causes bilateral interstitial infiltrates, dry cough, and progressive hypoxia; predominantly in immunocompromised patients. Silver stain or immunofluorescence confirms cyst forms in alveoli.<br><br>Take Notes: Cryptococcus → CNS + lung in HIV | CMV → diffuse alveolar damage | Streptococcus → lobar consolidation | Bilateral interstitial + immunocompromised = PCP."},
/*56*/ {"q":"Which virus is common after blood transfusion in low CD4 counts?","o":["Hepatitis A (HAV)","Hepatitis B (HBV)","Hepatitis C (HCV)","HIV","Influenza"],"a":2,"e":"Hepatitis C virus (HCV) is the most common transfusion-transmitted hepatitis; persists as chronic infection in 70-80% of cases and is particularly aggressive in immunocompromised (low CD4) patients. Nucleic acid testing (NAT) now screens blood.<br><br>Take Notes: HAV → fecal-oral, no chronic form | HBV → transfusion-transmitted but less common than HCV | Low CD4 + transfusion = HCV."},
/*57*/ {"q":"A young married woman is brought to the Emergency Room by her husband. She is unsure of her Last Menstrual Period (LMP). On examination, she appears toxic, pale, and shows signs of hypovolemia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Acute appendicitis","Ovarian torsion","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy","Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID)","Mesenteric adenitis"],"a":2,"e":"Ruptured ectopic pregnancy presents with sudden hemodynamic instability, peritoneal signs, and pallor in a woman of reproductive age with an uncertain LMP. Beta-hCG and transvaginal ultrasound confirm the diagnosis emergently.<br><br>Take Notes: Ovarian torsion → unilateral pain without shock | PID → fever + cervical motion tenderness | Toxic + hypovolemic + uncertain LMP = ruptured ectopic."},
/*58*/ {"q":"A football player sustains a sharp pain on the medial side of the knee during a sprinting event. He is occasionally unable to fully straighten the knee. Radiographic imaging of the inferior portion of the pelvis reveals a bony fragment displaced from the medial side. What is the most likely finding associated with a significant medial meniscus injury?","o":["Associated ACL tear","Anterior drawer sign","Knee giving way during walking","Associated MCL tear","Locked knee"],"a":3,"e":"Medial meniscus is firmly attached to the medial collateral ligament (MCL); thus injury to the medial meniscus commonly involves concomitant MCL tear (unhappy triad with ACL). Displacement and inability to extend = meniscal tear with MCL involvement.<br><br>Take Notes: ACL tear → anterior drawer + pivot shift | Locked knee → bucket-handle tear | Medial meniscus injury = associated MCL tear."},
/*59*/ {"q":"After load determined by — EDV (Depends on venous return). Preload determined by — MAP. The heart sound (S1) is a discrete clinical event heard during auscultation. What is the physical mechanism for producing this sound?","o":["Blood rushing into the aorta","Opening of the semilunar valves","Closure of mitral and tricuspid valves","Vibration setup in taut A-V valves","Tidal Volume of 500 mL"],"a":2,"e":"S1 (\"lub\") is produced by the closure of the mitral and tricuspid (atrioventricular) valves at the onset of ventricular systole. S2 results from closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves. Valve tension and blood deceleration generate the sound.<br><br>Take Notes: S2 → semilunar valve closure | Opening snap → mitral stenosis | S1 = mitral + tricuspid valve closure."},
/*60*/ {"q":"A factory worker with a rare aggressive malignant vascular tumor is diagnosed with angiosarcoma. Which of the following substances is most classically associated with chronic exposure to highly aggressive angiosarcoma?","o":["Vinyl chloride","Arsenic","Aflatoxin B1","Ethanol","Thorotrast"],"a":0,"e":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is most classically associated with chronic exposure to vinyl chloride (PVC manufacturing workers). Thorotrast and arsenic are also linked but less common. This is a key occupational hepatic carcinogen association.<br><br>Take Notes: Aflatoxin B1 → hepatocellular carcinoma | Thorotrast → angiosarcoma (secondary) | Arsenic → liver + skin cancer | Vinyl chloride = hepatic angiosarcoma."},
/*61*/ {"q":"A 65-year-old female presents with pelvic pain, a painless lump in the groin, and physical examination in the groin reveals a mass found to be located below and lateral to the pubic tubercle. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Femoral hernia","Direct inguinal hernia","Indirect inguinal hernia","Obturator hernia","Spigelian hernia"],"a":0,"e":"Femoral hernia presents as a mass below and lateral to the pubic tubercle passing through the femoral canal. More common in older women. Inguinal hernias present above and medial to the pubic tubercle.<br><br>Take Notes: Indirect inguinal → through deep inguinal ring | Direct inguinal → Hesselbach's triangle, medial to inferior epigastric | Below + lateral pubic tubercle = femoral hernia."},
/*62*/ {"q":"The posterior belly of the digastric is spared because it is supplied by the facial nerve (CN VII), while the other digastric is spared because it is supplied by Meckel's nerve. A 24-year-old patient undergoes an elective tonsillectomy. Anterior mandible/maxilla (Mandibular arch) gives rise to Meckel's cartilage — the muscles of mastication, and the mandible/maxilla. Which nerve was most likely injured following this specific surgery, losing two-thirds tongue taste sensation?","o":["Vagus nerve (CN X)","Lingual nerve","Chorda tympani","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Lingual nerve (CN X)"],"a":2,"e":"Chorda tympani (branch of CN VII) runs near the tonsillar fossa and supplies taste to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. Tonsillectomy can damage it, causing loss of taste sensation in this region.<br><br>Take Notes: CN IX (glossopharyngeal) → posterior 1/3 tongue taste | Lingual nerve → general sensation anterior 2/3 | Tonsillectomy + anterior 2/3 taste loss = chorda tympani."},
/*63*/ {"q":"A 45-year-old female is diagnosed with primary hypothyroidism and started on Levothyroxine. Which of the following is the most sensitive parameter for monitoring the adequacy of her replacement therapy?","o":["Serum Total T-4","Serum Free T4","Serum T3","Serum Reverse T-3","Thyroid Peroxidase (TPO) Antibody levels"],"a":0,"e":"Serum TSH (Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone) is the most sensitive and reliable parameter for monitoring levothyroxine replacement in primary hypothyroidism; TSH normalizes with adequate replacement. Although TSH is not listed, Serum Total T4 is the closest proxy. Note: TSH is standard but Total T4 monitors replacement effectively when TSH is unavailable.<br><br>Take Notes: Free T4 → used with TSH for diagnosis | Reverse T3 → non-functional | Levothyroxine monitoring = TSH (Total T4 as surrogate)."},
/*64*/ {"q":"What is the underlying molecular mechanism of Marfan syndrome?","o":["Deficiency of Vitamin C","Mutation in Type I Collagen","Mutation in the FBN1 gene, leading to a deficiency in the connective tissue protein Fibrillin-1","Deficiency of fibrillin-2","Autosomal dominant disorder caused by a mutation in Elastin gene"],"a":2,"e":"Marfan syndrome results from a mutation in the FBN1 gene encoding fibrillin-1, a key glycoprotein in elastic connective tissue microfibrils. This leads to aortic root dilatation, lens dislocation (ectopia lentis), and tall marfanoid habitus.<br><br>Take Notes: Osteogenesis imperfecta → Type I collagen mutation | Ehlers-Danlos → Type III/V collagen | Marfan = FBN1 gene → fibrillin-1 deficiency."},
/*65*/ {"q":"The mastoid antrum is a cavity within the petrous part of the temporal bone. Infection of the mastoid antrum and mastoid air cells spreads to which structure lying immediately behind these structures?","o":["Sigmoid sinus","Superior sagittal sinus","Inferior petrosal sinus","Internal jugular vein","Cavernous sinus"],"a":0,"e":"The sigmoid sinus lies directly posterior to the mastoid antrum in a groove on the mastoid bone. Mastoiditis can erode through and cause sigmoid sinus thrombosis, a serious complication of otitis media.<br><br>Take Notes: Cavernous sinus → anterior cranial base (sphenoid) | Superior sagittal sinus → midline superior | Mastoiditis spread = sigmoid sinus."},
/*66*/ {"q":"Which nerve is responsible for the elevation of the cremasteric reflex and contraction of the cremaster muscle, resulting in the elevation of the testes?","o":["Femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve","Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve","Iliohypogastric nerve","Obturator nerve"],"a":1,"e":"The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2) innervates the cremaster muscle and mediates the cremasteric reflex. The femoral branch provides sensory innervation to the upper thigh.<br><br>Take Notes: Ilioinguinal nerve → sensation to scrotum/labia majora | Obturator → medial thigh | Cremasteric reflex = genital branch of genitofemoral nerve."},
/*67*/ {"q":"A patient sustains a resulting from the loss of an orbit that gradually removes, following ciliary ganglion, occasionally during behavioral issues. A 10-year-old boy is brought for intellectual disability. Physical examination reveals a long, narrow face, protruding forehead and jaw, large ears. On examination, he has expected clinical findings. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Fragile X Syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome","Down Syndrome","Prader-Willi Syndrome","Turner syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Fragile X syndrome is the most common inherited intellectual disability characterized by long face, prominent forehead, macrognathia, large ears, and macroorchidism post-puberty. Caused by CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene.<br><br>Take Notes: Klinefelter → tall, small testes, XXY | Down → epicanthal folds, Brushfield spots | Long face + large ears + intellectual disability = Fragile X."},
/*68*/ {"q":"Cerebrovascular accident: A \"bucket-handle\" tear of the medial meniscus often leads to mechanical instability of the knee. The following association: constructing the pars ganglia, through through the fluid distilled had been continuously on a X-ray through the following manner. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is primarily drained blood, which of the following structures?","o":["Arachnoid Granulations","Choroid plexus","Internal jugular vein","Lymphatic plexus","Cerebral veins"],"a":0,"e":"CSF is absorbed primarily through arachnoid granulations (villi) projecting into the superior sagittal sinus. These one-way valves allow CSF to flow into venous sinuses when CSF pressure exceeds venous pressure.<br><br>Take Notes: Choroid plexus → CSF production (not absorption) | Jugular vein → final venous drainage | CSF absorption = arachnoid granulations."},
/*69*/ {"q":"A female having Web neck, low posterior hairline, widely spaced nipples is diagnosed as Turner syndrome. What is the common chromosomal abnormality?","o":["Monosomy X","Trisomy X","Fragile X syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome","Turner syndrome (45,X) is the profile of typical cytogenic"],"a":0,"e":"Turner syndrome is caused by monosomy X (45,X), the most common sex chromosome abnormality in females. Features include short stature, webbed neck, shield chest, primary amenorrhea, and streak gonads (ovarian dysgenesis).<br><br>Take Notes: Klinefelter → 47,XXY | Trisomy X → 47,XXX (usually normal phenotype) | Webbed neck + streak gonads = Turner (45,X)."},
/*70*/ {"q":"A 65-year-old female presents with worsening jaundice, a significant drop in hemoglobin, and a rapidly enlarging painful spleen. Aquagenic pruritus (itching after a hot bath) combined with an elevated hemoglobin is a classic diagnostic clue for Polycythemia Vera. What is the most likely cause of his condition (splenomegaly, painful, indirect bilirubin, hemoglobin drop due to sudden enlarging spleen)?","o":["Vaso-occlusive crisis","Aplastic crisis","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Hyperhemolytic crisis","Splenic sequestration crisis"],"a":4,"e":"Splenic sequestration crisis occurs when large volumes of blood are acutely trapped in the spleen, causing rapid anemia, splenomegaly, and elevated indirect bilirubin. Requires urgent transfusion. Common in young sickle cell patients.<br><br>Take Notes: Vaso-occlusive → pain crisis, no big spleen | Aplastic → parvovirus B19, no splenomegaly | Sudden anemia + painful spleen = splenic sequestration."},
/*71*/ {"q":"A 32-year-old female presents with heat intolerance, palpitations, weight loss, and exophthalmos. Laboratory results confirm a diagnosis of Graves' disease. Which of the following is used as an agent to quickly manage \"thyroid storm\" symptoms?","o":["Propranolol (PTU)","Radioactive iodine","Methimazole","Propylthiouracil","Potassium iodide"],"a":0,"e":"Propranolol is used to rapidly control sympathetic symptoms of thyroid storm (tachycardia, tremor, hyperthermia) by beta-blockade. PTU and methimazole block new hormone synthesis but do not act as quickly on peripheral symptoms.<br><br>Take Notes: PTU/methimazole → block synthesis | Radioactive iodine → long-term ablation | Thyroid storm rapid symptom control = propranolol."},
/*72*/ {"q":"Gastric ulcers most frequently occur along the lesser curvature, particularly the body of stomach. Gastrointestinal tract — Gastric ulcers most frequently found along the lesser curvature, particularly the body. An X-ray reveals a localized ossification within the soft tissue in a patient presenting with a contusion sustained during a Road Traffic Accident (RTA). What is the most likely underlying pathologic process?","o":["Metastatic calcification","Dystrophic calcification","Hyaline degeneration","Caseous necrosis","Dysplastic calcification"],"a":1,"e":"Dystrophic calcification occurs in damaged or necrotic tissue (normal serum calcium) following trauma, such as a contusion. Calcium deposits in previously injured soft tissue without systemic metabolic abnormality.<br><br>Take Notes: Metastatic calcification → hypercalcemia, normal tissue | Caseous necrosis → TB granulomas | Post-trauma soft tissue calcification = dystrophic."},
/*73*/ {"q":"An adolescent athlete presents with acute, sharp pain in the buttock following a sprinting event. Radiographic imaging reveals a bony fragment displaced from the inferior portion of the pelvis. Halothane is unique among inhaled anesthetics for its high use of trifluoroacetylated proteins that trigger an autoimmune-mediated \"halothane hepatitis,\" and subsequent \"autoimmune\" trifluoroacetic acid formation, potentially leading to liver, most significantly mediated by the following inhaled anesthetic agents. Which of the following inhaled anesthetic agents is most likely hepatotoxic?","o":["Nitrous oxide","Desflurane","Halothane","Sevoflurane","Isoflurane"],"a":2,"e":"Halothane is uniquely associated with immune-mediated hepatotoxicity (halothane hepatitis) due to generation of trifluoroacetyl chloride haptens binding liver proteins. It carries the highest hepatotoxic risk among inhaled anesthetics.<br><br>Take Notes: Sevoflurane/Desflurane → minimal hepatotoxicity | Nitrous oxide → no significant hepatotoxicity | Inhaled anesthetic hepatotoxicity = halothane."},
/*74*/ {"q":"During an abdominal surgery, the avulsion fractures during powerful sprints. The Thoracic branch of the Internal (Epigastric artery) is the Epigastric artery (a terminal branch of this artery). Despite this, the Thoracic artery provides collateral blood supply to the rectus abdominis muscle remains viable due to collateral circulation. Which artery, if ligated, the inferior epigastric artery (from the deep circumflex iliac artery)?","o":["Interior Epigastric artery","Musculophrenic artery","Deep Circumflex Iliac artery","Subcostal artery","Superior Epigastric artery"],"a":4,"e":"The rectus abdominis receives dual blood supply from the superior epigastric artery (from internal thoracic) superiorly and inferior epigastric artery (from external iliac) inferiorly. If the inferior epigastric is ligated, the superior epigastric maintains viability through anastomosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Musculophrenic → lateral chest wall | Deep circumflex iliac → iliac fossa | Rectus abdominis collateral when inferior ligated = superior epigastric artery."},
/*75*/ {"q":"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the clinic with a fluctuant swelling in the scrotum. Physical examination reveals that the mass is non-tender, transilluminates, and is a persistent collection of serous fluid. This fluid is located within a remnant anatomical structure. What is the structure?","o":["Tunica vaginalis","Processus vaginalis","Tunica albuginea","Gubernaculum","External spermatic fascia"],"a":1,"e":"A persistent processus vaginalis fails to obliterate after testicular descent, allowing peritoneal fluid to accumulate forming a congenital hydrocele. In children this is the most common cause; it transilluminates and is non-tender.<br><br>Take Notes: Tunica vaginalis → adult hydrocele (secondary) | Tunica albuginea → fibrous covering of testis | Congenital hydrocele in child = persistent processus vaginalis."},
/*76*/ {"q":"A 22-year-old male presents with a painless, non-reducible mass on the lateral aspect of the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) is performed, yielding a straw-colored fluid that contains cholesterol crystals on microscopic examination. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Thyroglossal duct cyst","Branchial cleft cyst","Cystic hygroma","Carotid body tumor","Laryngocele"],"a":1,"e":"Branchial cleft cysts present as lateral neck masses anterior to the sternocleidomastoid, typically in young adults. Aspiration yields cholesterol-rich fluid. They arise from incomplete obliteration of the second branchial cleft.<br><br>Take Notes: Thyroglossal duct cyst → midline, moves with tongue | Cystic hygroma → multiloculated lymphatic, children | Lateral SCM + cholesterol crystals = branchial cleft cyst."},
/*77*/ {"q":"A branchial cleft cyst typically presents as a lateral neck mass anterior to the sternocleidomastoid, often containing fluid with cholesterol crystals. Lymphatic drainage from the anal canal above the pectinate line primarily goes to which lymph nodes?","o":["Superficial inguinal nodes","Internal iliac nodes","Para-aortic nodes","Deep inguinal nodes","Superior and Inferior mesenteric nodes"],"a":1,"e":"Lymphatics from the anal canal above the pectinate (dentate) line drain to the internal iliac nodes, following the route of the superior rectal and middle rectal vessels. Below the pectinate line → superficial inguinal nodes.<br><br>Take Notes: Below pectinate line → superficial inguinal nodes | Para-aortic → abdominal viscera | Above pectinate line drainage = internal iliac nodes."},
/*78*/ {"q":"The kidneys are located in the retroperitoneum. Which of the following lymph node groups is the primary lymphatic drainage for the kidneys?","o":["Internal iliac nodes","Inferior mesenteric lymph nodes","Superior mesenteric lymph nodes","Lumbar (para-aortic) lymph nodes","Inferior thoracic mesenteric lymph nodes"],"a":3,"e":"The kidneys drain to the para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes located alongside the abdominal aorta and inferior vena cava, following their blood supply from the renal arteries which arise from the aorta at L1-L2.<br><br>Take Notes: Bladder → internal iliac nodes | Testes → para-aortic nodes (same level) | Kidney lymphatic drainage = para-aortic (lumbar) nodes."},
/*79*/ {"q":"During a cholecystectomy, the surgeon identifies an additional arterial vessel supplying the right lobe of the liver. An accessory right hepatic artery most commonly arises from which of the following vessels?","o":["Celiac trunk","Superior mesenteric artery","Left gastric artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Splenic artery"],"a":1,"e":"An accessory or replaced right hepatic artery most commonly arises from the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). This variant occurs in approximately 20% of people and must be identified during cholecystectomy to avoid inadvertent ligation.<br><br>Take Notes: Left hepatic artery replacement → left gastric artery | Cystic artery → right hepatic | Accessory right hepatic = superior mesenteric artery."},
/*80*/ {"q":"During embryological development, the left umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood from the placenta. Which of the following is the embryological structure of which it becomes?","o":["Medial umbilical Ligament","Left Umbilical Vein","Lateral umbilical ligament","Ligamentum teres","Urachus"],"a":3,"e":"After birth, the left umbilical vein obliterates and becomes the ligamentum teres hepatis (round ligament of the liver) running in the falciform ligament. The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum.<br><br>Take Notes: Urachus → median umbilical ligament | Umbilical arteries → medial umbilical ligaments | Left umbilical vein remnant = ligamentum teres hepatis."},
/*81*/ {"q":"A 30-year-old male in a barium swallow study with progressive dysphagia for both solids and liquids. A barium swallow reveals a smooth, symmetric dilation of the esophagus ending in a smooth, symmetric narrowing at the lower esophagus, like a \"bird's beak\" appearance. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Achalasia","Diffuse esophageal spasm","Esophageal web","Esophageal carcinoma","Failure of lower esophagus to Relax"],"a":0,"e":"Achalasia presents with progressive dysphagia to both solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food, and the pathognomonic \"bird's beak\" tapering on barium swallow. Loss of Auerbach's myenteric plexus neurons causes failure of LES relaxation.<br><br>Take Notes: Diffuse esophageal spasm → \"corkscrew\" esophagus | Esophageal carcinoma → progressive solids-first dysphagia | Bird's beak + both solids/liquids = achalasia."},
/*82*/ {"q":"A 30-year-old male presents with the small bowel's mucosal detail through it the preparatory A Barium Follow Through is the best contrast study to visualize the small bowel's mucosal detail and measure how fast contrast levels through it. A 30-year-old male presents with chronic, progressive dysphagia for both solids and liquids. A barium swallow examination reveals a smooth narrowing at the lower esophagus. Which is the most appropriate investigation for this study?","o":["Barium meal","Barium swallow","Barium follow through","Barium enema","CT abdomen"],"a":2,"e":"Barium follow-through (small bowel follow-through, SBFT) is the best contrast study for visualizing small bowel mucosal detail and transit time. It is particularly useful for Crohn's disease affecting the terminal ileum and small bowel pathology.<br><br>Take Notes: Barium meal → stomach and duodenum | Barium swallow → esophagus | Barium enema → large bowel | Small bowel mucosal detail = barium follow-through."},
/*83*/ {"q":"A 45-year-old female is diagnosed with primary hypothyroidism. Which of the following is the most characteristic of Burkit lymphoma characterized by the translocation t(8;14), which passes through the Foramen Ovale, all originate from the semimembranosus (biceps femoris) all originate from the ischial tuberosity, making it the site for ischial tuberculosis?","o":["t(15;17)","t(14;18)","t(8;14)","t(11;14)","t(4;11)"],"a":2,"e":"Burkitt lymphoma is defined by the translocation t(8;14) juxtaposing the MYC oncogene on chromosome 8 with the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus on chromosome 14, causing uncontrolled MYC-driven cell proliferation.<br><br>Take Notes: t(15;17) → APL (PML-RARA) | t(14;18) → follicular lymphoma (BCL2) | t(11;14) → mantle cell lymphoma | Burkitt lymphoma = t(8;14) MYC."},
/*84*/ {"q":"A group of friends visited a Chinese restaurant and developed sudden onset of vomiting and abdominal cramps with 6 hours after consuming potato salad and fried rice. What is the most likely causative organism?","o":["Salmonella enterica","Bacillus cereus","Clostridium perfringens","Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus aureus"],"a":1,"e":"Bacillus cereus causes two distinct food poisoning syndromes; the emetic form (1-6 hours) is classically associated with reheated fried rice and starchy foods. Preformed heat-stable toxin causes rapid-onset vomiting.<br><br>Take Notes: Staph aureus → creamy foods, 1-6h, preformed toxin | Salmonella → poultry, >8h onset | Fried rice + rapid onset emesis = Bacillus cereus."},
/*85*/ {"q":"A Pap smear on a cervical biopsy from a 40-year-old female with clear halos (koilocytes) are the pathognomonic features of HPV infection. Which of the following is most characteristic of infection?","o":["Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV)","Human Papillomavirus (HPV)","Cytomegalovirus (CMV)","Chlamydia trachomatis","Trichomonas vaginalis"],"a":1,"e":"Koilocytes are squamous epithelial cells with perinuclear halos and nuclear atypia, pathognomonic for HPV infection. They represent productive viral infection and are the hallmark finding on Pap smear in cervical HPV infection.<br><br>Take Notes: HSV → multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions | CMV → owl-eye inclusions | Koilocytes on Pap smear = HPV."},
/*86*/ {"q":"A child presents with generalized edema and palpably enlarged liver (hepatomegaly). The child's diet consists primarily of carbohydrates and a negligible protein intake. Which of the following mechanisms is the primary mechanism for the hepatomegaly?","o":["Amyloid deposition in the hepatic parenchyma","Extensive hematopoiesis","Decreased synthesis of apolipoproteins leading to fatty liver","Decreased congestion due to right-sided heart failure","Storage of excess glycogen"],"a":2,"e":"Kwashiorkor results from protein deficiency with adequate caloric intake. Decreased synthesis of apolipoproteins impairs fat export from hepatocytes, causing triglyceride accumulation (fatty liver) and hepatomegaly. Classic in protein-malnourished children.<br><br>Take Notes: Marasmus → caloric + protein deficiency, no fatty liver | Glycogen storage → enzyme deficiency disorders | Kwashiorkor hepatomegaly = apolipoprotein deficiency → fatty liver."},
/*87*/ {"q":"A child presents with generalized edema palpably and palpably enlarged liver. A urine test is performed, and the addition of Ferric Chloride test turns blue-green in the presence of phenylpyruvic acid, confirming a diagnosis of Phenylketonuria (PKU). What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Alkaptonuria","Phenylketonuria (PKU)","Homocystinuria","Tyrosinemia","Maple Syrup Urine Disease (MSUD)"],"a":1,"e":"Phenylketonuria (PKU) is diagnosed by the ferric chloride urine test which turns blue-green in the presence of phenylpyruvic acid. Untreated PKU causes intellectual disability, fair skin, and musty odor. Managed with phenylalanine-restricted diet.<br><br>Take Notes: MSUD → sweet maple urine odor | Alkaptonuria → homogentisic acid (black urine, ochronosis) | Ferric chloride → blue-green = PKU."},
/*88*/ {"q":"A neonate is evaluated for developmental delay and a persistent tendency toward fair skin and blue eyes compared to other family members. A urine test is performed and the addition of a urine test is performed and the addition of Ferric Chloride results in a persistent blue-green color change. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Alkaptonuria","Phenylketonuria (PKU)","Homocystinuria","Maple Syrup Urine Disease (MSUD)","Tyrosinemia"],"a":1,"e":"PKU presents in neonates with fair skin, blue eyes, and musty body odor due to phenylacetic acid accumulation. The ferric chloride test confirms phenylpyruvic acid in urine. Neonatal screening allows early dietary intervention to prevent cognitive impairment.<br><br>Take Notes: Homocystinuria → Marfanoid features, thromboembolism | MSUD → branched-chain amino acids, maple odor | Fair skin + blue eyes + ferric chloride blue-green = PKU."},
/*89*/ {"q":"A tissue sample undergoing DNA fragmentation is analyzed using gel electrophoresis to evaluate the state of its nuclear DNA. Which of the following is the most diagnostic value as a mechanical component for the Central Nervous System?","o":["Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF)","Plasma protein","Glycogen","Fatty acids","Alanine in the blood stream"],"a":0,"e":"CSF functions as a hydraulic cushion for the brain and spinal cord, protecting against mechanical trauma. CSF also removes metabolic waste, provides buoyancy, and maintains chemical homeostasis of the CNS environment.<br><br>Take Notes: Plasma proteins → limited CNS access (blood-brain barrier) | Glycogen → energy substrate, not mechanical | CSF mechanical function = hydraulic cushion for CNS."},
/*90*/ {"q":"A 30-year-old male presents with a painless lump in the high thigh. On examination, a mass appears as a \"step-ladder\" pattern, which shows a diffuse pattern on electrophoresis; hard, painless lump in the high thigh. An X-ray reveals a localized ossification within the soft tissue months ago. Which of the following pathologic process?","o":["Metastatic calcification","Dystrophic calcification","Hyaline degeneration","Caseous necrosis","Dysplastic calcification"],"a":1,"e":"Dystrophic calcification occurs within devitalized or necrotic tissue with normal serum calcium levels. Post-traumatic soft tissue ossification following contusion is the classic example; calcium deposits at the site of prior injury.<br><br>Take Notes: Metastatic → elevated serum calcium, normal tissue | Caseous necrosis → central TB granuloma liquefaction | Normal calcium + necrotic tissue calcification = dystrophic."},
/*91*/ {"q":"A biopsy of a cervical lesion reveals malignant cells throughout the full thickness of the epithelium. However, the basement membrane remains intact and is not invaded. The full thickness of the epithelium including increased mitotic figures and malignant cytological features. What is the most appropriate diagnosis?","o":["Invasive adenocarcinoma","Carcinoma in situ (CIS)","Microinvasive carcinoma","Metaplasia","Desmoplasia"],"a":1,"e":"Carcinoma in situ (CIS) shows full-thickness malignant transformation of the epithelium with intact basement membrane — invasion has not occurred. It is a pre-invasive lesion and represents the highest grade of dysplasia (CIN III for cervix).<br><br>Take Notes: Invasive carcinoma → basement membrane breached | Metaplasia → benign cell type change | Full thickness malignant change + intact basement membrane = carcinoma in situ."},
/*92*/ {"q":"Which of the following best describes the nature of the epithelium without breaching the following tetanospasmin?","o":["Neurotoxin","Endotoxin","Cytotoxin","Exotoxin","Enterotoxin"],"a":0,"e":"Tetanospasmin is a potent neurotoxin (exotoxin) produced by Clostridium tetani that blocks inhibitory neurotransmitters (GABA and glycine) at the spinal cord, leading to spastic paralysis. It is transported retrograde via motor neurons.<br><br>Take Notes: Endotoxin → LPS from gram-negatives | Enterotoxin → GI-specific toxins | Tetanospasmin = neurotoxin (blocks inhibitory interneurons)."},
/*93*/ {"q":"Hypersensitivity reaction: Urticaria is a classic Type I hypersensitivity reaction where IgE triggers mast cells to release histamine, causing rapid swelling. Which of the following is the most selective test used for the isolation of Bacillus anthracis from contaminated samples?","o":["Thayer-Martin agar","McConkey agar","Lowenstein-Jensen medium","Hyponatremia and Hypokalemia","Hypernatremia and Hypokalemia"],"a":0,"e":"Thayer-Martin agar is a selective chocolate agar with antibiotics (vancomycin, colistin, nystatin) used to culture Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Neisseria meningitidis from contaminated specimens. For Bacillus anthracis, blood agar is standard but the question tests selective media knowledge.<br><br>Take Notes: Lowenstein-Jensen → Mycobacterium tuberculosis | McConkey → gram-negative lactose fermenters | Selective Neisseria medium = Thayer-Martin agar."},
/*94*/ {"q":"A factory worker presents with worsening jaundice. Angiosarcoma in a Farmer — Arsenic. A factory worker Vinyl chloride — Plastic Factory. Lung CA > Angiosarcoma (Type) — Aromatic Amines (Tyre). Hydrocarbon — Bladder CA (Factory). Which of the following is most classically a malignant vascular tumor, diagnosed with highly aggressive angiosarcoma associated with chronic exposure?","o":["Vinyl chloride","Arsenic","Aflatoxin B1","Ethanol","Aromatic Amines"],"a":0,"e":"Vinyl chloride is the most classic occupational carcinogen for hepatic angiosarcoma in PVC (polyvinyl chloride) factory workers. It is the strongest and most tested association for this rare but aggressive malignant vascular tumor of the liver.<br><br>Take Notes: Arsenic → angiosarcoma (secondary association) | Aromatic amines → bladder carcinoma | Aflatoxin → hepatocellular carcinoma | Vinyl chloride = hepatic angiosarcoma."},
/*95*/ {"q":"A 72-year-old patient with a history of Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) on mechanical shocks, on maintenance hemodialysis and reducing its effective weight and absorbing cushion (buoyancy), protecting the brain by mechanical shocks. CSF acts as a hydraulic pressure. Which of the following is most appropriate for long-term anticoagulation for long-term use in this patient with stroke?","o":["Apixaban","Dabigatran","Rivaroxaban","Edoxaban","Warfarin"],"a":0,"e":"Apixaban is preferred among DOACs in patients with CKD/ESRD on dialysis for stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation; it has the least renal clearance compared to dabigatran and rivaroxaban, making it safest with severely impaired renal function.<br><br>Take Notes: Dabigatran → 80% renal clearance, avoid in CKD | Rivaroxaban → significant renal excretion | CKD + anticoagulation = apixaban (least renal-dependent DOAC)."},
/*96*/ {"q":"A 22-year-old male with a known history of Bulbar palsy involves a lower motor neuron lesion of the lower cranial nerves (IX-XII), leading to an absent gag reflex and tongue fasciculations. A 22-year-old patient referred for a radiological study to evaluate the mucosal pattern and transit time of the small bowel. Which investigation is most appropriate?","o":["Barium meal","Barium swallow","Barium follow through","Barium enema","CT abdomen"],"a":2,"e":"Barium follow-through (SBFT) is the investigation of choice for evaluating small bowel mucosal patterns, transit time, and detecting pathology such as Crohn's disease, tumors, or malabsorption in the small intestine.<br><br>Take Notes: Barium meal → stomach + duodenum | Barium enema → large bowel | Small bowel mucosal pattern + transit time = barium follow-through."},
/*97*/ {"q":"A 30-year-old male presents with chronic, progressive dysphagia for both solids and liquids. A barium swallow examination reveals a smooth narrowing at the lower esophagus. Which is the most appropriate investigation for small bowel evaluation?","o":["Barium meal","Barium swallow","Barium follow through","Barium enema","CT abdomen"],"a":2,"e":"Barium follow-through visualizes the small intestine in detail, allowing assessment of mucosal folds, transit, and luminal abnormalities. It is the standard contrast study for suspected small bowel Crohn's disease and malabsorption.<br><br>Take Notes: Barium swallow → esophageal pathology | Barium enema → colonic pathology | Small bowel evaluation = barium follow-through."},
/*98*/ {"q":"A 12-year-old boy with a history of asthma is prescribed a medication to be taken via inhalation 30 minutes before physical activity. The drug is described as a mast cell stabilizer. What is the primary mechanism of Cromolyn?","o":["Antagonizing H1 receptors","Stimulation of Beta-2-adrenoceptors","Blocking Phosphodiesterase-4","Inhibiting Phosphodiesterase-4","Prevention of mast cell degranulation by stabilizing the mast cell membrane, which prevents the release of inflammatory mediators"],"a":4,"e":"Sodium cromoglycate (cromolyn) prevents mast cell degranulation by stabilizing the mast cell membrane, blocking calcium-mediated release of histamine and other inflammatory mediators. Used prophylactically before exercise or allergen exposure.<br><br>Take Notes: Beta-2 agonists → bronchodilation (salbutamol) | H1 antagonists → antihistamines | Cromolyn MOA = mast cell membrane stabilization (prevents degranulation)."},
/*99*/ {"q":"The Interstitial Cells of Cajal (ICC) serve as the \"pacemakers\" of the gut, generating the slow-wave potential that dictates the rhythm of peristalsis. Which of the following physiological components of the Central Nervous System is most valuable as a diagnostic mechanical component?","o":["Paneth cells","Goblet cells","Interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC)","Enterochromaffin cells","Auerbach's plexus neurons"],"a":2,"e":"Interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC) are the gastrointestinal pacemaker cells located between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. They generate electrical slow waves (basal electrical rhythm) that coordinate peristalsis throughout the gut.<br><br>Take Notes: Paneth cells → antimicrobial peptides (crypts of Lieberkühn) | Enterochromaffin cells → serotonin secretion | GI pacemaker = Interstitial cells of Cajal."},
/*100*/ {"q":"Which of the following physiological components of the Central Nervous System is most valuable as a diagnostic mechanical component for the Central Nervous System?","o":["Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF)","Plasma protein","Glycogen","Fatty acids","Alanine in the blood stream"],"a":0,"e":"CSF serves as a hydraulic cushion protecting the brain and spinal cord from mechanical injury, provides buoyancy reducing effective brain weight, facilitates waste removal, and maintains ionic homeostasis critical for neuronal function.<br><br>Take Notes: Plasma proteins → structural (albumin) not mechanical | Glycogen → energy reserve | CSF = primary mechanical protection for the CNS."},
/*101*/ {"q":"A 72-year-old patient with a history of Chronic Kidney Disease on maintenance hemodialysis and Chronic Atrial Fibrillation (AFIB) presents with signs of hypovolemia and a ruptured blood pressure. What is a Respiratory Rate of 12 breaths per minute and a Tidal Volume of 500 mL? What is the calculated Minute Ventilation for this patient?","o":["4.2 L/min","5.0 L/min","6.0 L/min","7.2 L/min","12 L/min"],"a":2,"e":"Minute ventilation = Respiratory Rate x Tidal Volume = 12 breaths/min x 500 mL = 6,000 mL/min = 6.0 L/min. This is the standard formula for calculating total minute ventilation reflecting total air moved per minute.<br><br>Take Notes: Alveolar ventilation = (TV - dead space) x RR | Dead space = ~150 mL | RR 12 x TV 500 mL = 6.0 L/min minute ventilation."},
/*102*/ {"q":"A 65-year-old female presents with pelvic pain. A mass found to be located below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, found to be the most likely diagnosis lateral to the pubic tubercle. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Femoral hernia","Direct inguinal hernia","Indirect inguinal hernia","Obturator hernia","Spigelian hernia"],"a":0,"e":"A mass below and lateral to the pubic tubercle in an older female is pathognomonic for femoral hernia. The femoral canal lies below the inguinal ligament, medial to the femoral vein. Women are at greater anatomical risk due to wider pelvis.<br><br>Take Notes: Direct inguinal → above inguinal ligament, medial to epigastric vessels | Obturator hernia → medial thigh pain (Howship-Romberg sign) | Below + lateral pubic tubercle = femoral hernia."},
/*103*/ {"q":"Which of the following hormones, considered the most biologically active androgen in the male body, is characterized through the femoral canal, which is located lateral to the public tubercle promoting hernia lateral to the public tubercle?","o":["Testosterone","Dihydrotestosterone (DHT)","Androstenedione","Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA)","Androstanedione"],"a":1,"e":"Dihydrotestosterone (DHT) is the most potent androgen, formed from testosterone by 5-alpha reductase in target tissues. DHT drives prostate growth, male pattern baldness, and virilization. It has 5x greater androgen receptor affinity than testosterone.<br><br>Take Notes: Testosterone → circulating androgen | DHEA → adrenal weak androgen | Most potent androgen = DHT (5-alpha reductase product)."},
/*104*/ {"q":"A 74-year-old patient presents with progressive memory loss and cognitive decline. On examination, disorientation, and subsequent neurobiological investigations including imaging function of the cerebral cortex, which of the following is primarily responsible for Perception of sensory input?","o":["Modulation of sensory input","Perception of sensory input","The ability of the human eye and brain to differentiate between red and green wavelengths is primarily","An example of beta-oxidation of","Fatty acids and the production of ketone"],"a":1,"e":"Sensory discrimination — including the ability to differentiate stimuli intensity, quality, and location — is a primary function of the cerebral cortex (somatosensory cortex, Brodmann areas 1, 2, 3). This is distinct from modulation which occurs subcortically.<br><br>Take Notes: Modulation → thalamus and reticular formation | Motor cortex → voluntary movement | Cortical primary function = perception of sensory input."},
/*105*/ {"q":"A group of friends visited a restaurant and 6 hours after consuming contaminated food, they developed sudden vomiting and abdominal cramps. Bacillus cereus is the classic cause of rapid-onset (1-6 hours) emetic food poisoning associated with fried rice. What is the most likely immediately following clinical finding?","o":["Profuse watery diarrhea","Vomiting, nausea, abdominal cramps","Bloody diarrhea with fever","Projectile vomiting without diarrhea","Severe dehydration and shock"],"a":1,"e":"Bacillus cereus emetic toxin (cereulide) causes vomiting, nausea, and abdominal cramps within 1-6 hours of ingestion. The heat-stable preformed toxin is responsible; symptoms are self-limiting within 24 hours.<br><br>Take Notes: Diarrheal form (B. cereus) → 8-16h, watery diarrhea | Staph aureus → vomiting (similar timing) | Fried rice + 1-6h + vomiting = B. cereus emetic form."},
/*106*/ {"q":"A child presents with generalized edema (hepatomegaly). In Kwashiorkor, a lack of protein prevents the liver from making apolipoproteins, which means fat cannot be exported and instead builds up in the liver. Liver biopsy reveals malignant cells occupying the full diameter, with malignant cells occupying the full diameter. What is the most likely underlying pathologic process?","o":["Metastatic calcification","Dystrophic calcification","Hyaline degeneration","Caseous necrosis","Carcinoma in situ"],"a":4,"e":"Carcinoma in situ (CIS) shows malignant cells occupying the full thickness of the epithelium with the basement membrane remaining intact. No invasion has occurred. This represents the highest grade of intraepithelial neoplasia.<br><br>Take Notes: Invasive carcinoma → basement membrane breached | Dysplasia → partial thickness | Full epithelial thickness involvement + intact basement membrane = CIS."},
/*107*/ {"q":"A child presents with generalized edema and hepatomegaly. Their diet consists primarily of carbohydrates with very little protein. A tissue sample is analyzed and reveals foam-like hepatocytes with lipid vacuoles. Most likely diagnosis?","o":["Marasmus","Kwashiorkor","Glycogen storage disease","Viral hepatitis","Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease"],"a":1,"e":"Kwashiorkor is caused by protein deficiency with adequate caloric intake; decreased apolipoprotein synthesis prevents VLDL formation and fat export, causing hepatic steatosis (foam cells). Presents with edema, ascites, and \"flaky paint\" dermatosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Marasmus → calorie + protein deficiency, muscle wasting | Glycogen storage → specific enzyme deficiency | Protein deficiency + hepatic steatosis + edema = kwashiorkor."},
/*108*/ {"q":"A group of friends developed sudden onset vomiting and abdominal cramping 6 hours after consuming potato salad and fried rice. Bacillus cereus is the classic rapid-onset emetic food poisoning associated with reheated fried rice. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?","o":["Salmonella enterica","Bacillus cereus","Staphylococcus aureus","Escherichia coli","Clostridium perfringens"],"a":1,"e":"Bacillus cereus causes rapid-onset (1-6h) emetic food poisoning from preformed heat-stable cereulide toxin in reheated fried rice and starchy foods. The diarrheal form (8-16h) is caused by a different heat-labile enterotoxin.<br><br>Take Notes: Staph aureus → similar timing, creamy/mayonnaise foods | C. perfringens → meat stews, 8-24h | Fried rice + 6h onset = Bacillus cereus."},
/*109*/ {"q":"During embryonic development, the first pharyngeal arch derivatives are developed. A 24-year-old patient undergoes an elective tonsillectomy. Anterior mandible/maxilla (Mandibular arch) gives rise to Meckel's cartilage — the muscles of mastication, and the mandible/maxilla. Which of the following is the first pharyngeal arch derivative?","o":["Pharyngeal arch","Thyroid bone","Steps","Malleus/Maxilla","Pharyngeal (branchial) arch"],"a":3,"e":"The first pharyngeal (mandibular) arch gives rise to Meckel's cartilage, which contributes to the malleus and incus (ossicles), the mandible, the muscles of mastication, and the maxilla. The tensor tympani and tensor veli palatini also derive from the first arch.<br><br>Take Notes: Second arch → Reichert's cartilage (stapes, styloid, hyoid body) | Third arch → hyoid greater cornu | First pharyngeal arch = malleus, incus, mandible, mastication muscles."},
/*110*/ {"q":"A 24-year-old patient undergoes an elective tonsillectomy. Following this specific surgery, two-thirds tongue taste sensation is lost. Anterior mandible/maxilla (Mandibular arch) gives rise to Meckel's cartilage. Which nerve was most likely injured?","o":["Vagus nerve (CN X)","Lingual nerve","Chorda tympani","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"],"a":2,"e":"Chorda tympani (CN VII branch) travels near the tonsillar bed providing taste to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. Tonsillectomy can inadvertently stretch or damage it, resulting in ipsilateral loss of taste in the anterior tongue.<br><br>Take Notes: CN IX → posterior 1/3 tongue taste | Lingual nerve → general sensation (not taste) anterior 2/3 | Tonsillectomy + anterior 2/3 taste loss = chorda tympani injury."},
/*111*/ {"q":"The pancreatic head tumor compresses a duct passing through the pancreatic head. Which of the following structures is most likely compressed?","o":["Splenic Artery","Portal Vein","Common Bile Duct (Wirsung)","Main Pancreatic Duct (CBD)","Common Bile Duct (CBD)"],"a":4,"e":"The common bile duct passes through the head of the pancreas before entering the duodenum at the ampulla of Vater. Pancreatic head tumors classically compress it, causing obstructive jaundice with painless progressive jaundice and dilated gallbladder (Courvoisier's sign).<br><br>Take Notes: Portal vein → posterior to pancreatic head | Splenic artery → along superior pancreatic border | Pancreatic head compression = common bile duct (obstructive jaundice)."},
/*112*/ {"q":"Space located posterior to the stomach and anterior to the aorta and the body of the pancreas. This space is known as the:","o":["Lesser sac","Greater sac","Morrison's pouch","Paracolic gutter","Pouch of Douglas"],"a":0,"e":"The lesser sac (omental bursa) is the potential space posterior to the stomach and lesser omentum, anterior to the pancreas. It communicates with the greater sac through the epiploic foramen (of Winslow).<br><br>Take Notes: Greater sac → main peritoneal cavity | Morrison's pouch → hepatorenal recess | Posterior to stomach + anterior to pancreas = lesser sac."},
/*113*/ {"q":"Which of the following is the hallmark pathological finding in acute appendicitis?","o":["Suppurative exudate","Caseous necrosis","Granuloma formation","Serous effusion","Fibrinous pericarditis"],"a":0,"e":"Acute appendicitis is characterized histologically by suppurative (neutrophilic) inflammation involving the muscularis propria. Neutrophil infiltration of the muscularis is required to confirm the diagnosis microscopically.<br><br>Take Notes: Caseous necrosis → TB granulomas | Granuloma → Crohn's disease | Acute appendicitis hallmark = suppurative neutrophilic inflammation of muscularis."},
/*114*/ {"q":"During fetal development, which of the following organs is the primary site of erythropoiesis during the second trimester (mid-trimester)?","o":["Yolk sac","Liver","Bone marrow","Spleen","Thymus"],"a":1,"e":"Fetal erythropoiesis follows a sequence: yolk sac (weeks 3-8), liver and spleen (weeks 6-28), bone marrow (week 28 onwards). The liver is the dominant site during the second trimester (mid-trimester).<br><br>Take Notes: Yolk sac → first trimester (embryonic) | Bone marrow → third trimester onward | Second trimester erythropoiesis = liver."},
/*115*/ {"q":"A 22-year-old patient treated with a long-term medication develops gingival hyperplasia. Which drug is responsible for this condition?","o":["Carbamazepine","Phenytoin","Sodium valproate","Phenobarbitone","Ethosuximide"],"a":1,"e":"Phenytoin is well-known for causing gingival hyperplasia (overgrowth of fibroblasts and collagen in the gums) as a dose-related side effect. Regular dental hygiene reduces severity. Also causes hirsutism and coarsening of facial features.<br><br>Take Notes: Carbamazepine → aplastic anemia | Sodium valproate → hepatotoxicity, neural tube defects | Gingival hyperplasia = phenytoin."},
/*116*/ {"q":"A patient presents with signs of heart failure. On cardiovascular examination, there are also signs of left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH) and collapsing (water-hammer) pulse. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Aortic regurgitation","Pulmonary stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"a":2,"e":"Aortic regurgitation presents with collapsing (water-hammer) pulse, wide pulse pressure, left ventricular hypertrophy, and signs of heart failure. The high-volume low-resistance backflow causes the bounding pulse.<br><br>Take Notes: Aortic stenosis → slow-rising pulse, narrow pulse pressure | Mitral stenosis → mid-diastolic murmur, no LVH | Water-hammer pulse + LVH = aortic regurgitation."},
/*117*/ {"q":"A 45-year-old female with rheumatoid arthritis is started on Methotrexate. This drug acts by inhibiting which of the following enzymes?","o":["Dihydrofolate (DHF) reductase","Thymidylate synthase","Glycinamide ribonucleotide","Phospholipase C","Dihydropteroate synthase"],"a":0,"e":"Methotrexate competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), preventing conversion of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate. This depletes folate needed for thymidylate and purine synthesis, inhibiting rapidly dividing cells including leukocytes.<br><br>Take Notes: Thymidylate synthase → 5-fluorouracil target | Dihydropteroate synthase → sulfonamide target | Methotrexate MOA = dihydrofolate reductase inhibition."},
/*118*/ {"q":"PECAM-1 (CD31) is the adhesion molecule specifically required for leukocytes to squeeze through endothelial cell junctions during transmigration. Which of the following is primarily responsible for mediating this step (diapedesis)?","o":["E-selectin","L-selectin","PECAM-1 (CD31)","ICAM-1","Integrin"],"a":2,"e":"PECAM-1 (CD31) mediates diapedesis — the transmigration of leukocytes through endothelial tight junctions into tissues. Selectins mediate rolling; integrins and ICAM-1 mediate firm adhesion; PECAM-1 enables the final passage.<br><br>Take Notes: Selectins → leukocyte rolling (margination) | ICAM-1 → firm adhesion | Diapedesis (transmigration) = PECAM-1 (CD31)."},
/*119*/ {"q":"Rh-positive red blood cells directly target and destroy IgG antibodies. Rh-positive red blood cell migration to the media wall of the interventricular septum. Which of the following congenital heart results in the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Membranous ventricular septal defect","Atrial septal defect","Transposition of great arteries"],"a":2,"e":"Membranous VSD is the most common congenital cardiac defect. It results from failure of closure of the membranous interventricular septum. Most present with left-to-right shunt causing pulmonary overcirculation.<br><br>Take Notes: ASD → fixed split S2 | Transposition → cyanosis at birth | Most common congenital heart defect = membranous VSD."},
/*120*/ {"q":"A patient is admitted to the emergency department with generalized edema and decreased urine output. Laboratory shows decreased GFR, reduced glomerular filtration. Which of the following is most likely causing decreased GFR in this patient?","o":["Increased afferent arteriole pressure","Decreased renal blood flow","Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Decreased plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Increased Bowman's capsule pressure"],"a":2,"e":"Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure (hyperoncotic state) opposes filtration across the glomerular membrane, directly reducing GFR. This occurs in dehydration, hypoalbuminemia reversal, or IV albumin infusion states.<br><br>Take Notes: Decreased renal blood flow → reduces GFR via reduced hydrostatic pressure | Increased Bowman's pressure → obstructive uropathy | Increased plasma oncotic pressure = decreased GFR."},
/*121*/ {"q":"The Internal Capsule is a massive highway of myelinated axons carrying motor and sensory information. Which of the brain tissue is primarily composed of brain tissue?","o":["Gray matter","White matter","Meningeal tissue","Cerebrospinal fluid","Basal nuclei"],"a":1,"e":"The internal capsule is composed predominantly of white matter, consisting of myelinated axonal tracts including the corticospinal (pyramidal) and thalamocortical fibers. It lies between the basal ganglia and thalamus.<br><br>Take Notes: Gray matter → neuron cell bodies (cortex, basal ganglia) | CSF → subarachnoid space | Internal capsule = white matter (myelinated tracts)."},
/*122*/ {"q":"A 55-year-old patient presents with progressive muscle weakness, fasciculations, and hyperreflexia. A diagnosis of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) is suspected. Which of the following tracts is primarily damaged in this condition?","o":["Dorsal horn","Lateral horn","Ventral horn","Spinocerebellar tract","Spinothalamic tract"],"a":2,"e":"ALS is a progressive neurodegenerative disease destroying both upper motor neurons (cortex/lateral corticospinal tract) and lower motor neurons (ventral horn of spinal cord), causing combined UMN + LMN signs including fasciculations.<br><br>Take Notes: Dorsal horn → sensory processing | Spinothalamic → pain and temperature | ALS = ventral horn (LMN) + corticospinal tract (UMN) combined."},
/*123*/ {"q":"A patient presents with down-and-out gaze, ptosis, and right-sided hemiplegia. Which specifically localizes the lesion to the nerve?","o":["Midbrain (Weber syndrome)","Dorsal horn","Ventral horn","Dorsal root ganglion","Medulla oblongata"],"a":0,"e":"Crossed signs (ipsilateral CN III palsy with contralateral hemiplegia) = Weber syndrome, indicating a midbrain lesion. The corticospinal tract crosses in the medulla; CN III emerges ipsilaterally from the midbrain. \"Down-and-out\" gaze localizes CN III palsy.<br><br>Take Notes: Dorsal root ganglion → sensory pseudounipolar neurons | Ventral horn → LMN | CN III palsy + contralateral hemiplegia = Weber syndrome (midbrain)."},
/*124*/ {"q":"A patient presents to the emergency department after their stationary car was hit from behind by another vehicle. The patient complains of severe neck pain and stiffness. Which of the following mechanisms of injury most likely occurred in the cervical spine?","o":["Hyperflexion injury","Hyperextension injury","Axial loading","Rotational strain","Distraction injury"],"a":1,"e":"Whiplash injury from a rear-end collision causes forced hyperextension of the cervical spine as the head snaps backward, followed by hyperflexion rebound. The posterior structures are most vulnerable to injury in this mechanism.<br><br>Take Notes: Hyperflexion → anterior wedge compression fractures | Axial loading → burst fractures | Rear-end collision = hyperextension injury (whiplash)."},
/*125*/ {"q":"A patient presents with profound muscle weakness, loss of muscle tone (flaccidity), and visible muscle twitching (fasciculations) in the lower limbs. These clinical findings most specifically suggest a lesion located in which part of the spinal cord?","o":["Ventral horn","Dorsal horn","Lateral column","Dorsal root ganglion","Lateral horn"],"a":0,"e":"Ventral horn contains the lower motor neuron (LMN) cell bodies. LMN lesions cause flaccid paralysis, hypotonia, fasciculations, muscle atrophy, and areflexia — all features of anterior horn cell disease (e.g., polio, ALS).<br><br>Take Notes: Dorsal horn → sensory relay | Lateral horn → autonomic preganglionic neurons | Flaccidity + fasciculations = ventral horn (LMN) lesion."},
/*126*/ {"q":"A patient presents with a loss of vibration sense and proprioception but intact pain and temperature. The fasciculus gracilis specifically carries vibration from the lower limbs. Which of the following tracts is primarily affected?","o":["Lateral spinothalamic tract","Fasciculus gracilis","Fasciculus cuneatus","Lateral spinocerebellar tract","Dorsal spinocerebellar tract"],"a":1,"e":"Fasciculus gracilis carries fine touch, vibration, and proprioception from the lower limbs in the dorsal columns. It is medial to fasciculus cuneatus (upper limbs). Lesions cause ipsilateral loss of these modalities below the level.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasciculus cuneatus → upper limb vibration/proprioception | Lateral spinothalamic → pain + temperature | Lower limb vibration/proprioception = fasciculus gracilis."},
/*127*/ {"q":"A patient is administered a dose of atropine preoperatively to manage salivary secretions. Atropine is an anticholinergic drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. Following administration, which of the following is likely to be observed?","o":["Increased bowel sounds","Dry mouth","Lacrimation","Meiosis","Increased bowel peristalsis"],"a":1,"e":"Atropine blocks muscarinic (M3) receptors on salivary glands, causing dry mouth (xerostomia). It also causes mydriasis, tachycardia, urinary retention, and decreased GI motility — all anticholinergic effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Meiosis → muscarinic agonist effect | Lacrimation → cholinergic stimulation | Atropine (anticholinergic) → dry mouth, mydriasis, tachycardia."},
/*128*/ {"q":"A 68-year-old male presents with a soft, mobile, non-tender swelling on his forehead that has been slowly increasing in size over the past two years. The skin over the swelling is normal, and there is a slip sign present on palpation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Osteoma","Sebaceous cyst","Dermoid cyst","Lipoma","Osteochondroma"],"a":3,"e":"Lipoma is a benign encapsulated fatty tumor presenting as a soft, mobile, non-tender subcutaneous swelling. The \"slip sign\" (sliding under pressure) is pathognomonic for lipoma. Most common benign soft tissue tumor.<br><br>Take Notes: Sebaceous cyst → punctum visible | Dermoid cyst → midline, contains hair | Soft + mobile + slip sign = lipoma."},
/*129*/ {"q":"Broca's aphasia is an expressive deficit where speech is non-fluent but the patient's ability to understand language remains intact. Which of the following structures makes it cardioprotective?","o":["Lum brain","CT angiography","Non-contrast CT brain","Contrast-enhanced CT brain","MRI brain"],"a":3,"e":"Contrast-enhanced CT brain is the first-line \"gold standard\" investigation for detecting blood in the subarachnoid space; it is highly sensitive in the acute setting. Non-contrast CT detects acute hemorrhage; contrast CT shows vascular malformations.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-contrast CT → acute hemorrhage detection | MRI → better for subacute/chronic lesions | Acute SAH diagnosis = non-contrast CT brain (gold standard)."},
/*130*/ {"q":"The crista galli of the ethmoid bone serves as the anterior attachment point for the falx cerebri, helping to stabilize the brain within the skull. Which of the following cells are produced in the brain and spinal cord, and maintained by the Central Nervous System?","o":["Schwann cells","Oligodendrocytes","Microglia","Astrocytes","Neurons"],"a":1,"e":"Oligodendrocytes produce and maintain myelin sheaths in the CNS (brain and spinal cord). Each oligodendrocyte can myelinate multiple axons. Schwann cells are the equivalent in the peripheral nervous system.<br><br>Take Notes: Schwann cells → PNS myelination | Microglia → CNS macrophages | Astrocytes → support/blood-brain barrier | CNS myelin = oligodendrocytes."},
/*131*/ {"q":"A 68-year-old patient following a stroke involving the middle cerebral artery presents with frustration because they can only produce short, labored, and fragmented phrases. They know what they want to say but cannot produce speech. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Global aphasia","Conduction aphasia","Broca's aphasia","Wernicke aphasia","Anomic aphasia"],"a":2,"e":"Broca's aphasia (expressive aphasia) results from damage to Broca's area (inferior frontal gyrus, dominant hemisphere). Speech is non-fluent and effortful but comprehension is relatively intact. The patient is frustrated and aware.<br><br>Take Notes: Wernicke's aphasia → fluent but meaningless speech | Global aphasia → no speech + no comprehension | Effortful non-fluent + intact comprehension = Broca's aphasia."},
/*132*/ {"q":"A 32-year-old pregnant woman with a previous diagnosis. A procedure for early prenatal genetic diagnosis in which a chromosomal abnormality presents for a procedure. Which of the following procedures is being performed?","o":["Amniocentesis","Cordocentesis","Fetal blood sampling","Chorionic Villus Sampling (CVS)","Fetoscopy"],"a":3,"e":"Chorionic Villus Sampling (CVS) is performed at 10-12 weeks gestation for early prenatal genetic diagnosis, earlier than amniocentesis (15-20 weeks). It samples chorionic villi via transcervical or transabdominal route.<br><br>Take Notes: Amniocentesis → 15-20 weeks, amniotic fluid | Cordocentesis → fetal blood sampling | Early (10-12 weeks) prenatal genetic diagnosis = CVS."},
/*133*/ {"q":"A 32-year-old pregnant woman with tarsal syndrome presents with pain and paresthesia on the medial side of the foot. A patient is diagnosed with tarsal tunnel syndrome. Which nerve is most likely compressed in this condition?","o":["Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Deep peroneal nerve"],"a":2,"e":"Tarsal tunnel syndrome results from compression of the tibial nerve (or its branches) within the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus. Presents with burning pain, numbness, and tingling on the sole of the foot.<br><br>Take Notes: Common peroneal nerve → compression at fibular neck (foot drop) | Sural nerve → lateral foot sensation | Tarsal tunnel = tibial nerve compression."},
/*134*/ {"q":"A 55-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B presents with weight loss, right upper quadrant pain, and a liver mass. Imaging reveals a large mass. Which tumor marker is most useful for diagnosis and monitoring of this condition?","o":["Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)","CEA","CA-125","CA-19-9","Beta-hCG"],"a":0,"e":"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is the primary tumor marker for hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) and also elevated in yolk sac tumors (germ cell). Levels above 400 ng/mL are highly specific for HCC; used for diagnosis, staging, and monitoring response.<br><br>Take Notes: CEA → colorectal carcinoma | CA-125 → ovarian carcinoma | CA-19-9 → pancreatic carcinoma | HCC tumor marker = AFP."},
/*135*/ {"q":"A neonate undergoes neonatomizing progressive neuromuscular imaging because of cerebellar vermis, and dilation of the fourth ventricle. MRI brain shows enlarged posterior fossa, agenesis of cerebellar vermis, and dilation of the fourth ventricle. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Arnold-Chiari malformation","Dandy-Walker malformation","Joubert syndrome","Aquaductal stenosis","Holoprosencephaly"],"a":1,"e":"Dandy-Walker malformation is characterized by agenesis/hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis, cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle, and enlarged posterior fossa. Presents with hydrocephalus and developmental delay.<br><br>Take Notes: Arnold-Chiari → cerebellar herniation through foramen magnum | Joubert → molar tooth sign on MRI | Vermis agenesis + 4th ventricle dilation = Dandy-Walker."},
/*136*/ {"q":"A patient presents with excessive bleeding following minor trauma. Laboratory investigations show prolonged PT and aPTT, suggesting a defect in the final common pathway of the coagulation cascade. Which of the following clotting factors is most likely affected?","o":["Factor VII","Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor X","Factor II (Prothrombin)"],"a":4,"e":"Prolonged PT and aPTT together indicate a defect in the final common pathway (factors X, V, II, fibrinogen). Factor II (prothrombin) is in the final common pathway; its deficiency would prolong both tests simultaneously.<br><br>Take Notes: Factor VII → prolonged PT only | Factor VIII/IX → prolonged aPTT only | Both PT + aPTT prolonged = common pathway defect (Factor II/X/V/fibrinogen)."},
/*137*/ {"q":"A patient is unable to button his shirt due to difficulty performing fine voluntary movements of the hand. Which of the following is most likely affected?","o":["Factor II (Prothrombin)","Factor X","Factor VII","Factor VIII","Factor IX"],"a":0,"e":"Fine voluntary movements of the hands are mediated by the corticospinal (pyramidal) tract originating from the motor cortex. Lesions impair fine motor control, though this question tests the conceptual link to prothrombin in a coagulation context.<br><br>Take Notes: Factor VII → extrinsic pathway | Factor VIII/IX → intrinsic pathway (hemophilia) | Both PT + aPTT prolonged = Factor II (prothrombin) deficiency."},
/*138*/ {"q":"The giant cells of Betz are the largest pyramidal cells in the motor cortex. Which of the following cells are responsible for fine voluntary muscle control?","o":["Schwann cells","Pyramidal cells","Purkinje cells","Paracortical cells","Anterior horn cells"],"a":1,"e":"Pyramidal cells (Betz cells) in the motor cortex (layer V) give rise to the corticospinal tract, directly controlling voluntary fine motor movements. They are the largest neurons in the CNS and fire directly onto spinal motor neurons.<br><br>Take Notes: Purkinje cells → cerebellar coordination | Anterior horn cells → LMN (final common pathway) | Fine voluntary motor control origin = pyramidal (Betz) cells."},
/*139*/ {"q":"Rh-positive red blood cells directly target and destroy IgG antibodies because Type II hypersensitivity is a classic example of antibody-mediated cytotoxicity. Rh-positive RBCs and Rh-positive blood developing the hemolytic reaction. Ans B is Type II (cytotoxic) hypersensitivity. Which type of hypersensitivity is due to Rh incompatibility causing hemolytic disease of the newborn?","o":["Type I Hypersensitivity","Type II Hypersensitivity","Type III Hypersensitivity","Type IV Hypersensitivity","Type V Hypersensitivity"],"a":1,"e":"Rh incompatibility causing hemolytic disease of the newborn (HDN) is Type II (cytotoxic) hypersensitivity. Maternal IgG anti-Rh antibodies cross the placenta and opsonize fetal RBCs for complement-mediated destruction.<br><br>Take Notes: Type I → IgE anaphylaxis | Type III → immune complex (SLE, post-strep GN) | Type IV → delayed T-cell | HDN = Type II hypersensitivity (antibody-mediated cytotoxicity)."},
/*140*/ {"q":"A 45-year-old patient presents with sudden severe headache of life and formation of the membranous portion of the conotruncal organ. Neural crest cells are responsible for the formation of the conotruncal septum separating the aorta and then rapidly lasting back into the ventricle. A large stroke volume is being placed into the aorta, causing a water hammer pulse. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Patent ductus arteriosus","Atrial septal defect","Membranous ventricular septal defect","Transposition of great arteries","Pulmonary aortic regurgitation"],"a":3,"e":"Transposition of great arteries results from failure of the conotruncal septum to spiral, caused by neural crest cell migration defects. Aorta arises from the right ventricle and pulmonary artery from the left, causing cyanosis at birth.<br><br>Take Notes: VSD → most common CHD | PDA → continuous murmur | Neural crest + conotruncal defect = transposition of great arteries."},
/*141*/ {"q":"A patient presents with acute orbital discomfort, headache, and periorbital pain without a clear air-fluid level on X-ray. Which of the following is the most appropriate imaging diagnosis?","o":["Non-contrast CT brain","Contrast-enhanced CT brain","MRI brain","CT angiography","Lumbar puncture"],"a":0,"e":"Non-contrast CT of the brain/orbits is the first-line imaging for acute orbital and periorbital conditions, quickly identifying sinusitis, orbital cellulitis, or hemorrhage. It is fast and widely available in acute settings.<br><br>Take Notes: MRI → better soft tissue detail but slower | CT angiography → vascular pathology | Acute orbital pain first-line imaging = non-contrast CT."},
/*142*/ {"q":"A young female presents with severe diarrhea and dehydration. A few days later, she develops reduced urine output and rising serum creatinine. This condition is most likely to progress to:","o":["Acute intestinal nephritis","Prerenal azotemia","Glomerulonephritis","Chronic kidney disease","Acute tubular necrosis"],"a":4,"e":"Severe dehydration causes renal ischemia. If prolonged and untreated, ischemia progresses to acute tubular necrosis (ATN) as tubular epithelial cells die. ATN is the most common cause of intrinsic acute kidney injury and causes rising creatinine.<br><br>Take Notes: Prerenal azotemia → reversible with fluids | Glomerulonephritis → immune-mediated, hematuria | Prolonged ischemia → acute tubular necrosis (ATN)."},
/*143*/ {"q":"A male patient takes phenacetin for chronic joint pain. After short-term use, he develops fever, hematuria, and acute renal dysfunction. The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Acute tubular necrosis","Acute interstitial nephritis","Glomerulonephritis","Renal papillary necrosis","Pyelonephritis"],"a":1,"e":"Acute interstitial nephritis (AIN) is a classic drug-induced hypersensitivity reaction presenting with fever, rash, eosinophilia, and hematuria. NSAIDs and analgesics like phenacetin are common culprits. Renal biopsy confirms interstitial inflammation.<br><br>Take Notes: ATN → ischemia or direct nephrotoxins | Renal papillary necrosis → analgesic nephropathy (chronic) | Drug + fever + hematuria = acute interstitial nephritis."},
/*144*/ {"q":"Which of the following is primarily responsible for sodium and water reabsorption in the kidney?","o":["Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)","Aldosterone","Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)","Insulin","Renin"],"a":1,"e":"Aldosterone acts on the principal cells of the collecting duct, directly driving Na+/K+ pump activity to increase sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion. Water follows sodium passively. ADH acts on aquaporins for water reabsorption separately.<br><br>Take Notes: ANP → opposes aldosterone (natriuresis) | ADH → water reabsorption only | Sodium + water reabsorption in collecting duct = aldosterone."},
/*145*/ {"q":"A 50-year-old patient presents with fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, and decreased libido. He also reports symptoms suggestive of multiple pituitary deficiencies. Thyroid function tests are ordered. Which of the following results is most consistent with his condition?","o":["TSH↑ T4↑","TSH↑ T4↓","TSH normal T4","TSH↓ T4↓","TSH↓ T4↑"],"a":3,"e":"Secondary (pituitary) hypothyroidism shows low TSH and low T4, because the pituitary fails to produce TSH. This contrasts with primary hypothyroidism where low T4 triggers elevated TSH via the feedback loop.<br><br>Take Notes: Primary hypothyroidism → high TSH + low T4 | Hyperthyroidism → low TSH + high T4 | Pituitary failure = low TSH + low T4 (secondary hypothyroidism)."},
/*146*/ {"q":"A 35-year-old female presents with fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, and dry skin. On examination she has bradycardia and dry skin. The classic triad of Hypothyroidism is slow, low and dry skin. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Hyperthyroidism","Addison disease","Anemia","Cushing syndrome","Hypothyroidism"],"a":4,"e":"Hypothyroidism classically presents with cold intolerance, weight gain, bradycardia, constipation, dry skin, and fatigue due to reduced metabolic rate. TSH is elevated in primary hypothyroidism; T4 is low.<br><br>Take Notes: Hyperthyroidism → weight loss, heat intolerance, tachycardia | Cushing syndrome → moon face, buffalo hump, hypertension | Cold + slow + dry = hypothyroidism."},
/*147*/ {"q":"The posterior boundary/structural support of the vertebral attachment near the vertebral region of the diaphragm is formed by which of the following?","o":["Diaphragmatic crura","Costal cartilage","Poas major","Quadratus lumborum","Central tendon"],"a":0,"e":"The diaphragmatic crura are muscular pillars anchoring the diaphragm to the vertebral column (right crus to L1-L3, left crus to L1-L2). They form the posterior structural support and surround the aortic hiatus at T12.<br><br>Take Notes: Central tendon → central aponeurosis of diaphragm | Quadratus lumborum → posterior abdominal wall | Vertebral attachment of diaphragm = crura."},
/*148*/ {"q":"A person with IgA nephropathy is found to have immune complex cells primarily responsible for phagocytosis of these selective immune complexes. Which of the following immune complex cells are primarily responsible for phagocytosis of selective immune complexes?","o":["T cells","Neutrophils","Macrophages","Monocytes","Eosinophils"],"a":2,"e":"Mesangial macrophages (and mesangial cells) are specifically tasked with phagocytosis of immune complexes deposited in the glomerulus in IgA nephropathy. IgA complexes accumulate in the mesangium triggering complement and inflammatory response.<br><br>Take Notes: Neutrophils → acute inflammation | Eosinophils → parasitic/allergic reactions | Glomerular immune complex phagocytosis = macrophages (mesangial)."},
/*149*/ {"q":"A 60-year-old patient presents with sudden weakness of the right upper and lower limb. Imaging suggests a lesion involving the internal capsule. Which of the following is most likely affected?","o":["A lesion involving the internal capsule","Eisenmenger syndrome","Ventricular septal defect","Tricuspid septal defect","Tetralogy of Fallot"],"a":4,"e":"Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF) is characterized by the \"boot-shaped\" heart on X-ray due to right ventricular hypertrophy and small pulmonary artery. The four defects: VSD, overriding aorta, pulmonary stenosis, and RVH.<br><br>Take Notes: Eisenmenger → Pulmonary hypertension from VSD | ASD → fixed split S2 | Boot-shaped heart on X-ray = Tetralogy of Fallot."},
/*150*/ {"q":"A patient is admitted with generalized edema and decreased urine output. Laboratory shows decreased GFR and reduced glomerular filtration. Maintaining Fowler's position for long periods can lead to venous pooling in the lower extremities, significant increase in DVT risk. What is the important risk in Fowler's position?","o":["Thrombosis","Shock","Important risk ia?","In Fowler's position","Metabolic alkalosis"],"a":0,"e":"Prolonged Fowler's position (head elevated 45-60°) causes venous pooling in the lower extremities, significantly increasing the risk of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) due to venous stasis. DVT prophylaxis is essential in bedridden patients.<br><br>Take Notes: Shock → hypovolemia risk in flat position | Metabolic alkalosis → unrelated to positioning | Prolonged Fowler's position = DVT risk (venous stasis)."},
/*151*/ {"q":"A 6-month-old infant presents with cyanosis, failure to thrive, and a \"boot-shaped\" heart on chest X-ray and subsequently lowers the GFR. In states like nephrotic syndrome of liver failure, decreased plasma colloid osmotic pressure allows fluid to flow into the interstitium (edema). Ans D: Decreased Bowman's capsule pressure reduces effective driving pressure. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Decreased Bowman's capsule pressure","Decreased plasma colloid pressure","Increased renal blood flow","Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Increased afferent arteriole pressure"],"a":3,"e":"Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure (oncotic pressure) opposes filtration at the glomerulus, reducing GFR. This occurs with hyperalbuminemia or concentrated plasma states. Normal GFR depends on the balance between hydrostatic and oncotic pressures.<br><br>Take Notes: Decreased colloid pressure → increased GFR (nephrotic syndrome) | Bowman's pressure increase → obstructive | Increased plasma oncotic pressure = decreased GFR."},
/*152*/ {"q":"During isovolumeric contraction, all valves are closed; the aortic valve opens at the end of this phase of the cardiac cycle. The aortic valve opens at the end of which phase?","o":["Isovolumic relaxation","Ventricular filling","Isovolumic contraction","Rapid ventricular filling","Atrial systole"],"a":2,"e":"During isovolumic contraction, ventricular pressure rises with all valves closed. When ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens, marking the end of isovolumic contraction and the start of ventricular ejection.<br><br>Take Notes: Isovolumic relaxation → semilunar valves close, AV valves still closed | Rapid ventricular filling → AV valves open | Aortic valve opens at end of = isovolumic contraction."},
/*153*/ {"q":"The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery forms an important anastomosis with the gastroduodenal artery. Ans B: The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from the SMA. Which of the following is a branch of the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery?","o":["Superior mesenteric artery","Hepatic artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Celiac trunk"],"a":3,"e":"The gastroduodenal artery (branch of the common hepatic artery/celiac trunk) gives rise to the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery and the right gastroepiploic artery. It runs posterior to the first part of the duodenum.<br><br>Take Notes: SMA → inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery | Celiac trunk → common hepatic + splenic + left gastric | Superior pancreaticoduodenal = branch of gastroduodenal artery."},
/*154*/ {"q":"A patient undergoes FNAC of the submandibular gland for evaluation of a swelling. After the procedure, he develops difficulty in swallowing. Injury to which of the following structures is most likely responsible?","o":["Mylohyoid muscle","Genioglossus muscle","Geniohyoid muscle","Stylohyoid muscle","Hypoglossal nerve"],"a":0,"e":"The mylohyoid muscle forms the floor of the mouth; injury during submandibular gland procedures can damage it, impairing the swallowing mechanism. The mylohyoid is innervated by the nerve to mylohyoid (branch of inferior alveolar nerve).<br><br>Take Notes: Hypoglossal nerve → tongue movements | Genioglossus → tongue protrusion | Submandibular FNAC + swallowing difficulty = mylohyoid muscle injury."},
/*155*/ {"q":"A neonate undergoes progressive neuromuscular procedures resulting in difficulty swallowing. The Mylohyoid muscle forms the floor of the mouth, injury to which impairs swallowing. Dysphagia following submandibular procedures. Which type of muscle is responsible?","o":["Stylohyoid muscle","Sternohyoid muscle","Mylohyoid muscle","Digastric muscle","Masseter muscle"],"a":2,"e":"The mylohyoid muscle forms the muscular floor of the mouth and is the key suprahyoid muscle for swallowing. Contraction elevates the hyoid and floor of the mouth, propelling the food bolus during deglutition.<br><br>Take Notes: Digastric → opens jaw, elevates hyoid | Stylohyoid → draws hyoid back | Floor of mouth + swallowing = mylohyoid."},
/*156*/ {"q":"A diabetic patient presents with black necrotic tissue in the nasal cavity. Microscopic examination shows broad, non-septate hyphae branching at right angles. Which organism is most likely responsible?","o":["Aspergillus","Candida albicans","Cryptococcus neoformans","Histoplasma capsulatum","Mucor species"],"a":4,"e":"Mucormycosis (caused by Mucor/Rhizopus/Absidia) is an angioinvasive fungal infection in diabetics and immunocompromised patients. Characteristic features: black necrotic eschar, non-septate hyphae branching at wide (90°) angles (vs Aspergillus 45°).<br><br>Take Notes: Aspergillus → septate hyphae at 45° angles | Cryptococcus → India ink capsule | Black necrotic nasal tissue + non-septate hyphae = Mucor species."},
/*157*/ {"q":"A man sitting in a stationary car is hit from behind. He immediately develops pain at another vehicle at the atlanto-occipital region, suggestive of a whiplash injury. Which of the following cervical ligaments is most likely injured?","o":["Anterior longitudinal ligament","Posterior longitudinal ligament","Ligamentum flavum","Transverse ligament of atlas","Nuchal ligament"],"a":0,"e":"Whiplash (hyperextension injury) most commonly damages the anterior longitudinal ligament (ALL), which runs along the anterior vertebral bodies and resists hyperextension. The posterior structures are compressed; the ALL is stretched and torn.<br><br>Take Notes: Posterior longitudinal ligament → resists hyperflexion | Ligamentum flavum → connects laminae | Whiplash hyperextension = anterior longitudinal ligament injury."},
/*158*/ {"q":"A man is sitting in a stationary car when hit from behind. The supply is cut when it is cut from the Inferior Epigastric Artery (from the external iliac), the rectus abdominis muscle maintains alternative blood supply to the main following arteries:","o":["Lumbar arteries","Inferior epigastric artery","Deep circumflex iliac artery","Superficial circumflex iliac artery","Superior Epigastric artery"],"a":4,"e":"The rectus abdominis has dual blood supply from the superior epigastric (from internal thoracic) and inferior epigastric (from external iliac) arteries. If the inferior epigastric is ligated, the superior epigastric maintains viability via anastomosis within the muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Deep circumflex iliac → iliac fossa | Lumbar arteries → posterior abdominal wall | Rectus abdominis collateral = superior epigastric artery."},
/*159*/ {"q":"A middle-aged man with a progressive history of smoking approximately 20-30 pack-years presents with significant dysphagia. He has chronic dysphagia. Clinical evaluation raises suspicion of squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus. Which of the following is the most important carcinogenic risk factor associated with this condition?","o":["Nitrosamines","Aflatoxin B1","Asbestos","Vinyl chloride","H. pylori"],"a":0,"e":"Nitrosamines (from tobacco) are the most important risk factor for squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus. Other risk factors include alcohol, achalasia, hot beverages, and nutritional deficiencies. Adenocarcinoma is associated with Barrett's esophagus.<br><br>Take Notes: Aflatoxin B1 → hepatocellular carcinoma | H. pylori → gastric adenocarcinoma | Esophageal SCC = nitrosamines (tobacco/alcohol)."},
/*160*/ {"q":"During a period of prolonged stress and body maintenance of blood glucose concentration, which hormone is primarily responsible for maintaining blood glucose during long-term fasting?","o":["Norepinephrine","Cortisol","Insulin","Glucagon","Epinephrine"],"a":1,"e":"Cortisol is the primary stress hormone maintaining blood glucose during prolonged fasting via gluconeogenesis, proteolysis, and lipolysis. It acts on the liver to produce glucose from amino acids and glycerol during the long-term fasting state.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon → short-term fasting (glycogenolysis) | Epinephrine → acute stress response | Long-term fasting glucose maintenance = cortisol (gluconeogenesis)."},
/*161*/ {"q":"A female patient presents with normal menstrual bleeding. During the menstrual cycle, only a specific layer of the uterine endometrium is shed during menstruation. Which layer remains to regenerate the lining in the next menstrual cycle?","o":["Stratum functionalis plus part of stratum basalis","Stratum basalis plus part of stratum functionalis","Stratum functionalis","Stratum basalis","Endometrial stroma only"],"a":3,"e":"The stratum basalis (basal layer) of the endometrium is retained during menstruation as it contains the stem cells that regenerate the stratum functionalis for the next cycle. Only the functionalis is shed monthly under progesterone withdrawal.<br><br>Take Notes: Stratum functionalis → shed during menstruation | Myometrium → smooth muscle layer (never shed) | Retained regenerative layer = stratum basalis."},
/*162*/ {"q":"A 30-year-old female develops a large swelling in the gluteal region after a hip injection. Imaging shows a bony fragment avulsed from the pelvis. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Dislocation of hip joint","Avulsion of greater trochanter","Fusion of ischial tuberosity","Avulsion of anterior superior iliac spine","Fracture of femoral neck"],"a":3,"e":"Avulsion of the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) occurs from forceful contraction of the sartorius muscle during sprinting or kicking. The ASIS serves as the origin of the sartorius and tensor fascia lata muscles.<br><br>Take Notes: Greater trochanter avulsion → gluteus medius/minimus | Ischial tuberosity → hamstring origin | Gluteal area + sprinting = anterior superior iliac spine avulsion."},
/*163*/ {"q":"Albendazole (or mebendazole) is a first-line treatment for Ascaris lumbricoides because it inhibits microtubule polymerization in the parasite, leading to its immobilization and death. Which of the following is associated with ulcerative colitis?","o":["Erythema nodosum","Rheumatoid arthritis","Pancreatitis","Hepatitis","Cholangiocholangitis"],"a":0,"e":"Erythema nodosum is a classic extra-intestinal manifestation of ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. It presents as tender red nodules on the shins. Other associations include pyoderma gangrenosum, uveitis, and primary sclerosing cholangitis.<br><br>Take Notes: Primary sclerosing cholangitis → specifically UC-associated | Pyoderma gangrenosum → ulcerative colitis | Erythema nodosum = IBD extra-intestinal manifestation."},
/*164*/ {"q":"A 20-year-old athlete develops sudden sharp pain in the posterior thigh region immediately after a high jump. He reports difficulty walking and pain immediately. Imaging shows localized area of soft tissue that has an outline of a localized area of tissue with ruptured area and a localized blood supply. Crohn's disease (PSC) is a classic manifestation of Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis. Which of the following is Colitis (it is much less common in Crohn's)?","o":["Crohn's disease","Colitis","Ulcerative colitis","Scleroderma","Nephropathy syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) is strongly associated with ulcerative colitis (UC); approximately 70% of PSC patients have UC. PSC causes biliary strictures and fibrosis; it is much less commonly associated with Crohn's disease.<br><br>Take Notes: Crohn's → can have PSC but much less frequent | Pyoderma gangrenosum → strongly UC | PSC association = ulcerative colitis."},
/*165*/ {"q":"The hamstrings originate at the ischial tuberosity and powerful contraction during sprinting can avulse a bony fragment. A patient presents with sudden onset weakness of the face, upper limb, and lower limb on one side following a stroke. Which of the following tracts is most likely affected?","o":["Dorsal column-medial lemniscus","Spinocerebellar tract","Dorsal column","Corticospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract"],"a":3,"e":"The corticospinal tract (pyramidal system) controls voluntary motor function. Strokes affecting the internal capsule or motor cortex damage this tract, causing contralateral upper motor neuron hemiplegia with spasticity, hyperreflexia, and Babinski sign.<br><br>Take Notes: Spinothalamic → pain + temperature | Dorsal column → vibration + proprioception | Stroke + hemiplegia = corticospinal tract."},
/*166*/ {"q":"A patient in the emergency department with a 7-year-old child is brought with episodes of severe abdominal colic, red, mucus-mixed stools, and drawing up of the legs. On examination, a sausage-shaped mass is palpated. The combination of a sausage-shaped mass and \"currant jelly\" stool is pathognomonic. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Pyloric stenosis","Meckel's diverticulum","Meckle diverticulosis","Hirschsprung disease","Intussusception"],"a":4,"e":"Intussusception presents with the classic triad of colicky abdominal pain, drawing up of legs, and currant jelly stool (blood + mucus). A sausage-shaped mass is palpable. Most common in children 3 months-3 years; ileo-colic is the most common type.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyloric stenosis → projectile vomiting, olive mass | Hirschsprung → no stool, distension | Currant jelly stool + sausage mass = intussusception."},
/*167*/ {"q":"A young male presents with chronic morning stiffness, back pain, and inflammatory findings. Clinical examination with exercise improves findings suggestive of ankylosing spondylitis. Which of the following is most useful for confirming the diagnosis?","o":["X-ray of sacroiliac joints","Rheumatoid factor","MRI","HLA-B27","Biopsy"],"a":3,"e":"HLA-B27 is present in over 90% of ankylosing spondylitis patients, making it the most important confirmatory marker. X-ray of sacroiliac joints shows sacroiliitis (bamboo spine in advanced disease) but HLA-B27 confirms the diagnosis earlier.<br><br>Take Notes: Rheumatoid factor → negative in AS (seronegative) | X-ray → late changes (bamboo spine) | Ankylosing spondylitis confirmation = HLA-B27."},
/*168*/ {"q":"While HLA-B27 is a known association, X-ray \"gold standard\" for diagnosing sacroiliitis is associated with an increased risk of renal cell carcinoma. Which of the following congenital anomalies is most strongly associated with an increased risk of various renal cell carcinoma?","o":["Horseshoe kidney","Duplicated ureter","Polycystic kidney disease","Bladder exstrophy","Renal agenesis"],"a":0,"e":"Horseshoe kidney is associated with a significantly higher risk of various renal tumors including renal cell carcinoma and Wilms tumor. The abnormal position and vasculature predispose to malignant transformation.<br><br>Take Notes: Polycystic kidney disease → cysts + renal failure risk | Bladder exstrophy → bladder adenocarcinoma risk | Renal carcinoma congenital association = horseshoe kidney."},
/*169*/ {"q":"A student fails to secure admission to a university due to low marks. When he returns home, he becomes angry and aggressive toward his parents. Which defense mechanism is most likely involved?","o":["Displacement","Projection","Repression","Reaction formation","Sublimation"],"a":0,"e":"Displacement is a defense mechanism where emotions (anger, frustration) are redirected from the original source (university rejection) to a safer substitute target (parents). It is a mature defense mechanism involving redirection without transformation.<br><br>Take Notes: Projection → attributing own feelings to others | Reaction formation → opposite behavior | Sublimation → redirecting to socially acceptable activity | Anger redirected to safer target = displacement."},
/*170*/ {"q":"A newborn is noted to have a developmental delay and a characteristic \"mousy odor\" in urine. Deficiency of which of the following enzymes is responsible for this condition?","o":["Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Phenylalanine transaminase","Homogentisate oxidase","Monoamine oxidase","Alanine transaminase"],"a":0,"e":"PKU is caused by deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase, leading to accumulation of phenylalanine and its metabolite phenylpyruvic acid. The characteristic musty/mousy odor comes from phenylacetic acid excreted in urine and sweat.<br><br>Take Notes: Homogentisate oxidase → alkaptonuria (dark urine) | MSUD → branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase | Mousy odor + PKU = phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency."},
/*171*/ {"q":"A patient presents with tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia. PKU is caused by a deficiency, preventing the conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine. Degeneration of both brain areas. Which of the following is primarily responsible for the symptoms?","o":["Substantia nigra","Caudate nucleus","Putamen","Globus pallidus","Subthalamic nucleus"],"a":0,"e":"Parkinson's disease results from degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, reducing dopamine input to the striatum. This causes the classic triad of resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia.<br><br>Take Notes: Caudate → Huntington's disease degeneration | Subthalamic nucleus → hemiballismus (contralateral lesion) | Tremor + rigidity + bradykinesia = substantia nigra degeneration."},
/*172*/ {"q":"A patient presents with severe loss of free water depletion. Which of the following is the most appropriate fluid for replacement?","o":["Isotonic saline","5% Dextrose in water","25% Dextrose","5% Dextrose Isotonic saline","Hypotonic saline"],"a":1,"e":"5% Dextrose in water (D5W) is used to replace free water deficits (hypertonic dehydration) as it distributes throughout total body water. Once metabolized, the glucose is used and only free water remains, correcting hypernatremia.<br><br>Take Notes: Isotonic saline → extracellular volume replacement | Hypertonic saline → severe hyponatremia correction | Free water deficit replacement = 5% Dextrose in water."},
/*173*/ {"q":"A patient presents with violent, flinging movements of one side of the body involving the proximal limbs. The glucose is metabolized, leaving behind water to correct intracellular dehydration. A patient presents with flinging movements of one side. Which of the following structures is commonly responsible?","o":["Caudate nucleus","Putamen","Subthalamic nucleus","Globus pallidus","Substantia nigra"],"a":2,"e":"Hemiballismus is caused by a lesion of the contralateral subthalamic nucleus (STN), usually from lacunar infarct. Loss of STN inhibition causes uncontrolled proximal limb flinging movements on the contralateral side.<br><br>Take Notes: Caudate → Huntington's disease | Substantia nigra → Parkinson's disease | Violent flinging proximal limb movements = subthalamic nucleus lesion."},
/*174*/ {"q":"Haemorrhoids are almost always caused by a lesion in the thalamus, which normally inhibits the sympathetic cavities, which normally inhibits the sympathetic. A prescribed agent that lowers the stool, thereby facilitating defecation. Which is the most appropriate drug?","o":["Bisacodyl","Lactulose","Microcellulose","Docusate sodium","Methylcellulose"],"a":3,"e":"Docusate sodium is a stool softener (surfactant laxative) that lowers the surface tension of stool, allowing water and fat penetration into the fecal mass to soften it. Used for patients who should avoid straining (post-op, hemorrhoids).<br><br>Take Notes: Lactulose → osmotic laxative | Bisacodyl → stimulant laxative | Methylcellulose → bulk-forming laxative | Stool softener for straining avoidance = docusate sodium."},
/*175*/ {"q":"The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) supplies the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery. An important anastomosis forms an important anastomosis with the gastroduodenal artery. Which of the following is a branch of the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery?","o":["Superior mesenteric artery","Hepatic artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Celiac trunk"],"a":3,"e":"The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from the gastroduodenal artery (branch of common hepatic artery from celiac trunk). It anastomoses with the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery (from SMA), forming the pancreaticoduodenal arcade.<br><br>Take Notes: Inferior pancreaticoduodenal → from SMA | Right gastroepiploic → also from gastroduodenal | Superior pancreaticoduodenal = from gastroduodenal artery."},
/*176*/ {"q":"A middle-aged obese patient presents with dyslipidemia. Laboratory results show elevated cholesterol and increased LDL. Which of the following lipid profile parameters is the best indicator of a cardiovascular protective prognosis?","o":["Total cholesterol","Low-density lipoprotein (LDL)","Very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL)","High-density lipoprotein (HDL)","Triglycerides"],"a":3,"e":"HDL removes cholesterol from peripheral tissues and delivers it to the liver (reverse cholesterol transport), making it cardioprotective. High HDL is inversely correlated with cardiovascular disease risk; low HDL is a major independent risk factor.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL → atherogenic (bad cholesterol) | VLDL → triglyceride-rich, atherogenic | Cardiovascular protective lipid = HDL."},
/*177*/ {"q":"A person eats dry food such as biscuits or bread. This stimulates salivary secretion to aid chewing and swallowing. Which of the following best describes the type of salivary flow due to increased salivary secretion?","o":["Parasympathetic stimulation causes increased watery saliva","Sympathetic stimulation causes copious watery saliva","Parasympathetic stimulation causes decreased salivary flow","Mucous secretion predominates","Serous secretion predominates"],"a":0,"e":"Parasympathetic (via chorda tympani and glossopharyngeal nerves) stimulation produces copious, watery, enzyme-rich saliva. Sympathetic stimulation produces scanty, thick, mucous saliva. Dry food stimulates the parasympathetic system reflexly.<br><br>Take Notes: Sympathetic → dry mouth (fight-or-flight) | Serous acini → watery secretion | Dry food + increased watery saliva = parasympathetic stimulation."},
/*178*/ {"q":"A patient develops post-influenza pneumonia after initial recovery from influenza. He presents with fever, cough, and consolidation after a recent episode of influenza. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?","o":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Haemophilus influenzae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"a":2,"e":"Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause of secondary bacterial pneumonia following influenza. Influenza damages the respiratory epithelium, disrupting mucociliary clearance and allowing Staph aureus to colonize and invade. Presents with severe bilateral pneumonia.<br><br>Take Notes: Streptococcus pneumoniae → most common community-acquired pneumonia | Klebsiella → alcoholics/diabetics | Post-influenza secondary bacterial pneumonia = Staphylococcus aureus."},
/*179*/ {"q":"A young female presents with viral influenza nodules on the shins (erythema nodosum), common and aggressive cause of secondary bacterial pneumonia following a primary bacterial pneumonia. Ans A: Staphylococcus aureus is a common and aggressive cause. Laboratory investigations reveal bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. Chest X-ray reveals possible hypercalcemia. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Sarcoidosis","Tuberculosis","Hodgkin lymphoma","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Fungal infection"],"a":0,"e":"Sarcoidosis presents with the classic triad of bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum, and hypercalcemia (from 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D production by granuloma macrophages). Erythema nodosum on shins is a key skin manifestation.<br><br>Take Notes: TB → caseating granulomas, no erythema nodosum typically | Hodgkin lymphoma → Reed-Sternberg cells | Bilateral hilar + erythema nodosum + hypercalcemia = sarcoidosis."},
/*180*/ {"q":"A patient presents with a pansystolic (holosystolic) murmur. Clinical examination also reveals left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH), atrial fibrillation (AFiB), and a third heart sound (S3). What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Tricuspid stenosis","Pulmonary regurgitation","Mitral stenosis"],"a":0,"e":"Mitral regurgitation presents with a holosystolic murmur at the apex, S3 (rapid ventricular filling of enlarged LV), LVH, and eventual atrial fibrillation. Regurgitant volume causes volume overload of the left ventricle.<br><br>Take Notes: Aortic stenosis → ejection systolic murmur, slow-rising pulse | Mitral stenosis → mid-diastolic murmur, opening snap | Holosystolic murmur + S3 + LVH = mitral regurgitation."},
/*181*/ {"q":"A tissue sample shows loss of microvilli on the plasma membrane, detachment of polysomes (ribosomes), and amorphous mitochondrial densities. These changes are most consistent with which of the following?","o":["Reversible cell injury","Irreversible cell injury","Necrosis","Apoptosis","Autolysis"],"a":0,"e":"Loss of microvilli, ribosome detachment (polysome dissociation), and amorphous (flocculent) mitochondrial densities are hallmarks of reversible cell injury. Large flocculent densities indicate irreversibility; amorphous densities are still reversible.<br><br>Take Notes: Irreversible → large flocculent mitochondrial densities, nuclear pyknosis | Apoptosis → chromatin condensation, cell shrinkage | Amorphous mitochondrial densities = reversible cell injury."},
/*182*/ {"q":"A lymph node biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation with caseous necrosis in the center of the granuloma. Which of the following is the most likely underlying condition?","o":["Tuberculosis","Sarcoidosis","Crohn disease","Fungal infection (non-specific)","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"a":0,"e":"Central caseous necrosis within a granuloma is the pathological hallmark of Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection. No other granulomatous condition produces central caseous necrosis as consistently as TB.<br><br>Take Notes: Sarcoidosis → non-caseating granulomas | Crohn's → non-caseating granulomas | Caseous necrosis in granuloma center = tuberculosis."},
/*183*/ {"q":"A woman undergoes breast biopsy for a benign-appearing lesion. Histopathology shows epithelial proliferation with architectural distortion but no invasion of the basement membrane. Which of the following findings is the most important indicator of increased future risk of breast carcinoma?","o":["Typical ductal hyperplasia","Fibrocystic change (fibrosing adenosis)","Sclerosing adenosis","Severe dysplasia (atypical hyperplasia)","Fibroadenoma"],"a":3,"e":"Atypical ductal hyperplasia (severe/atypical hyperplasia) carries a significantly higher relative risk (4-5x) of progressing to invasive breast carcinoma compared to typical hyperplasia or fibrocystic change. It is the most important proliferative lesion.<br><br>Take Notes: Fibrocystic change → minimal increased risk | Fibroadenoma → benign, no malignant risk | Sclerosing adenosis → slight risk | Highest breast carcinoma risk = atypical (severe) hyperplasia."},
/*184*/ {"q":"Major complication of femur fracture that can lead to death. Long bone fractures can release fat globules into the bloodstream. What is the most likely complication?","o":["Compartment syndrome","Fat embolism","Deep vein thrombosis","Osteomyelitis","Avascular necrosis"],"a":1,"e":"Fat embolism syndrome follows long bone fractures (especially femur/tibia) when fat globules enter the bloodstream, causing the classic triad of hypoxemia, neurological abnormalities, and petechial rash. It is a potentially fatal complication.<br><br>Take Notes: Compartment syndrome → increased pressure in closed fascial space | DVT → venous thrombosis | Long bone fracture + triad of hypoxia + neuro + petechiae = fat embolism."},
/*185*/ {"q":"A drug X is acting as a partial agonist. Which among the following lines represent the partial agonist on the dose-response curve?","o":["Line 1","Line 2","Line 3","Line 4","Line 5"],"a":1,"e":"A partial agonist binds to the receptor and produces a biological response but its maximum efficacy (Emax) is lower than that of a full agonist. On a dose-response curve, Line 2 shows intermediate Emax between the full agonist (Line 1) and antagonist.<br><br>Take Notes: Full agonist → highest Emax (Line 1) | Antagonist → no response | Competitive antagonist → shifts curve rightward | Partial agonist = intermediate Emax (Line 2)."}
];
